// Auto-generated offline chapter bundle for Junior Coders Book (scripts/build-books.js)
window.BOOK_CMD_CHAPTERS = {
  "README.md": "# Command Line & Terminal Mastery for Junior Coders 💻⚡\n\nWelcome to the official **Command Line Interface (CLI) Textbook** of the Junior Coders curriculum!\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Course Overview\n\nBefore graphic user interfaces (GUIs), windows, and mice existed, software engineers communicated with computers through text commands. Today, the Command Prompt and Terminal remain the fastest, most powerful way to:\n\n1. **Navigate the operating system** at lightning speed without touching a mouse.\n2. **Scaffold web applications** with multiple folders and files in a matter of seconds.\n3. **Run developer toolchains** including Git, Node.js, PHP, Composer, and local web servers.\n4. **Automate tasks** and manage server environments like a seasoned engineer.\n\nThis textbook equips students to be dual-fluent in both the **Windows Command Prompt (CMD)** and the **Unix / Git Bash / macOS Terminal**, building confidence from day one.\n\n---\n\n## 🗺️ Curriculum Roadmap\n\n| Part | Focus | Chapters |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Part 1** | Foundations & Orientation | Ch 1 - Ch 3 |\n| **Part 2** | Spatial Navigation Mastery | Ch 4 - Ch 6 |\n| **Part 3** | Managing Files & Folders | Ch 7 - Ch 9 |\n| **Part 4** | The Web Developer Workflow | Ch 10 - Ch 12 |\n| **Part 5** | Capstone Scaffolding Challenges | Ch 13 - Ch 14 |\n\n---\n\n## 🌟 Islamic Ethical Framework (*Amānah & Iḥsān*)\n\nIn accordance with our core guidelines:\n- **Digital Trust (*Amānah*)**: Destructive terminal commands (`del`, `rm -rf`) cannot be undone by the Recycle Bin. Power in the terminal comes with moral responsibility and carefulness.\n- **Never Blindly Paste**: We instill the habit of never running obfuscated or unknown scripts from unverified internet sources.\n- **Excellence (*Iḥsān*)**: Craft clean, well-organized directory trees and master shortcuts to avoid wasting time on repetitive tasks.\n- **Beneficial Knowledge (*'Ilm Nāfi'*)**: Harness terminal automation to build useful tools that benefit families, schools, and communities.\n",
  "part-01-foundations/ch01-what-is-the-terminal.md": "# Chapter 1: What is the Terminal? Demystifying the Black Window\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nAmina wants to borrow a book from the school library. She can walk along the shelves and look at every cover. Or she can go to the desk and say, \"Please give me *Science Experiments for Beginners*.\" The librarian brings it straight away. The terminal is like that library desk. You do not click and search. You ask for exactly what you want, in words.\n\n> **Key Idea**: The terminal is not a strange alien screen. It is **File Explorer without the mouse**. It is a direct line to your computer, where you give instructions by typing text instead of clicking.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain the difference between a GUI and a CLI.\n- Name the three terminals you will meet on a Windows computer.\n- Open Command Prompt and PowerShell, and close them safely.\n- Run your first harmless command and read what the screen prints.\n- Follow the four safety rules every terminal user keeps.\n\n## 🖱️ GUI vs. CLI: Two Ways to Talk to a Computer\n\nEvery day you use a **GUI**. GUI means **Graphical User Interface**. \"Graphical\" means it uses pictures.\n\n- You move a mouse pointer.\n- You double-click yellow folder icons.\n- You drag files into the Recycle Bin.\n- You right-click to see a menu.\n\nGUIs are friendly. But some jobs need hundreds of clicks. Imagine making 50 folders, one for each student in your class. That is a lot of clicking!\n\nThe **CLI** is the other way. CLI means **Command Line Interface**. People also call it the **terminal**, the **console** or the **command prompt**. It uses text only.\n\n- You type a short word called a **command** (for example `mkdir`, short for \"make directory\").\n- You press <kbd>Enter</kbd>.\n- The computer does the job in a moment.\n\n| | GUI (Graphical) | CLI (Command Line) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **You use** | Mouse, icons, windows | Keyboard and text |\n| **Speed for repeated jobs** | Slow | Very fast |\n| **Needs a screen?** | Yes | Can work on distant computers |\n| **Best for** | Browsing, drawing | Git, PHP, servers, tidy work |\n\n## 💡 Why Web Developers Learn the Terminal\n\nYou may ask: *\"I can already click. Why learn typing commands?\"* Here are four reasons.\n\n1. **Git**: Saving versions of your website and sharing them on GitHub happens in the terminal.\n2. **Local web servers**: To test a PHP website, you start a small server with a command.\n3. **Tool installers**: Tools like `npm` and `composer` download helpful code libraries. They are terminal tools.\n4. **Cloud servers**: Real websites run on computers far away. They have no mouse and no screen. You manage them by typing commands.\n\n## 🪟 The Three Terminals on Windows\n\nSchool labs usually use Windows. A Windows computer has three terminals you will meet.\n\n| Terminal | How to open it | Its prompt looks like |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Command Prompt (CMD)** | Press <kbd>Win</kbd> + <kbd>R</kbd>, type `cmd`, press <kbd>Enter</kbd> | `C:\\Users\\Amina>` |\n| **PowerShell** | Press <kbd>Win</kbd> + <kbd>R</kbd>, type `powershell`, press <kbd>Enter</kbd> | `PS C:\\Users\\Amina>` |\n| **Git Bash** | Search \"Git Bash\" in the Start menu (only if Git is installed) | `amina@LAB-PC MINGW64 ~` |\n\nIn this book, every command is shown for **CMD** and **PowerShell** side by side. Git Bash and macOS commands appear in a note. On a Mac, the program is called **Terminal**, and it works like Git Bash.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Never choose \"Run as administrator\".** An administrator terminal can change important system files. Everything in this book works in a normal window. If a menu offers \"Run as administrator\", pick the normal option.\n\n## ▶️ Your First Command: `ver` and `exit`\n\nLet us open a terminal and ask a harmless question. The command `ver` asks, \"Which Windows version is this?\" It changes nothing.\n\n**CMD** (type `ver`, then press <kbd>Enter</kbd>):\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>ver\n\nMicrosoft Windows [Version 10.0.26200.9550]\n\nC:\\Users\\Amina>\n```\n\nThe number on your computer may be different. That is fine.\n\n**PowerShell** does not know `ver`. It has its own way to ask:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\Users\\Amina> $PSVersionTable.PSVersion\n\nMajor  Minor  Build  Revision\n-----  -----  -----  --------\n5      1      26100  9549\n```\n\nAgain, your numbers may be different. Notice that after each command, the prompt comes back. The prompt coming back means: *\"Done. I am ready for the next instruction.\"*\n\nTo close either window, type `exit` and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>. The window closes. Nothing is printed.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS**: there is no `ver` command. Type `uname` to see the system name. Git Bash prints something like `MINGW64_NT-10.0-26200`. A Mac prints `Darwin`. Type `exit` to close.\n\n## 🤝 Safety and Trust (*Amānah*)\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> **No safety net.** When you delete a file from the terminal, it does **not** go to the Recycle Bin. It is gone.\n\nAs developers we keep **Amānah**, which means trust and care. Your computer and your files are a trust. Here are four rules.\n\n1. **Read before you press Enter.** Check every letter you typed.\n2. **Never paste commands you do not understand.** Some websites share harmful commands.\n3. **Never run the terminal as Administrator.** You do not need it.\n4. **Practise only in your own practice folder.** In this book that is `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice`. You will make it soon.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Typing a sentence instead of a command.** A beginner types English words and expects the computer to understand.\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>show my windows version\n'show' is not recognized as an internal or external command,\noperable program or batch file.\n```\n\nThe terminal only knows exact command words. Nothing was harmed. It just did not understand. The fixed version is the real command:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>ver\n\nMicrosoft Windows [Version 10.0.26200.9550]\n```\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nOpen **both** CMD and PowerShell, one after the other. In each one, look at the prompt. Write down the two prompts in your notebook. What is the same? What is different?\n\n*Hint*: Look at the very start of each prompt line. One terminal adds two extra letters.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- A **GUI** uses pictures and a mouse. A **CLI** uses typed commands.\n- Developers use the terminal for Git, servers, tool installers and cloud computers.\n- Windows has three terminals: CMD, PowerShell and Git Bash.\n- A command does its job, prints a result, and then the prompt comes back.\n- Terminal deletions skip the Recycle Bin. Never run as Administrator. Practise in your own folder.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\n1. What do **GUI** and **CLI** stand for?\n2. Give two reasons why web developers use the command line.\n3. What happens to a file you delete from the terminal?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. GUI = **Graphical User Interface**. CLI = **Command Line Interface**.\n2. Any two: Git, local web servers, tool installers (`npm`, `composer`), managing cloud servers.\n3. It is deleted straight away. It does **not** go to the Recycle Bin, so you cannot restore it from there.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\n1. Press <kbd>Win</kbd> + <kbd>R</kbd>, type `cmd`, and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>.\n2. Type `ver` and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>. What does the answer say?\n3. Type `exit` and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>. What happens?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. A Command Prompt window opens, showing a prompt like `C:\\Users\\Amina>`.\n2. A line like `Microsoft Windows [Version 10.0.26200.9550]`. Your numbers may differ.\n3. The window closes. `exit` does not print anything.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nYour teammate needs to set up a project. Compare two ways of explaining it:\n\n1. *\"Click the third button on the top right, then drag the blue box to the folder at the bottom.\"*\n2. *\"Type `mkdir images` and press Enter.\"*\n\nWhich instruction is less likely to go wrong? Give two reasons. How does this connect to *Iḥsān* (doing work with care and excellence)?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\nThe typed command is less likely to go wrong.\n\n- It is **exact**: every letter is written down. \"Third button\" can mean different buttons on different screens.\n- It can be **copied, checked and repeated** the same way every time, even on another computer.\n- It does not depend on window size, colours or where icons sit.\n\n*Iḥsān* means doing work carefully and well. Clear, exact instructions respect your teammate's time and avoid mistakes.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-01-foundations/ch02-anatomy-of-a-prompt.md": "# Chapter 2: The Anatomy of a Prompt & Command Grammar\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nBilal arrives at a big science fair. At the entrance there is a map with a red dot: **\"You are here.\"** Without that dot, the map is not much help. The terminal prompt is that red dot. It tells you where you are standing before you take a step. And when you ask a helper for something, you speak in a clear order: what to do, how to do it, and to what. Commands follow the same order.\n\n> **Key Idea**: Every prompt tells you **who you are** and **where you are standing**. Every command follows a simple sentence pattern: `command + options + target`.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Read each part of a CMD, PowerShell and Git Bash prompt.\n- Split any command into its command word, options and target.\n- Spot the different option styles: `/w`, `-Name` and `-l`.\n- Use quotation marks for names that contain spaces.\n\n## 🔎 Reading the Prompt\n\nWhen you open a terminal, you see a line of text and a blinking cursor. That line is the **prompt**.\n\n### In Command Prompt (CMD)\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>_\n```\n\nLet us break it into pieces.\n\n- **`C:`** is the **drive letter**. A drive is a storage space, like a hard disk. Most computers call the main one `C:`.\n- **`\\`** is the **path separator**. It goes between folder names. Windows uses the backslash `\\`.\n- **`Users\\Amina`** is the **current folder**. You are inside `Amina`, which is inside `Users`.\n- **`>`** means *\"I am ready for your instruction.\"*\n- **`_`** is the blinking **cursor**. Your typing appears here.\n\n### In PowerShell\n\n```text\nPS C:\\Users\\Amina> _\n```\n\nIt is almost the same. The letters **`PS`** at the start tell you this is PowerShell. That is helpful, because some commands work differently in each terminal.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS** prompts look different:\n> ```text\n> amina@LAB-PC MINGW64 ~\n> $ _\n> ```\n> `amina` is the user name and `LAB-PC` is the computer name. `~` (the **tilde**) is short for your **home folder**, such as `C:\\Users\\Amina`. The `$` sign means *\"ready\"*. Git Bash and macOS use forward slashes `/` between folders.\n\n## 🧩 Command Grammar: Command, Options, Target\n\nEvery instruction follows the same three-part pattern.\n\n```text\nCOMMAND     OPTIONS       TARGET\n(verb)      (how)         (what)\n```\n\n- The **command** is the action word. Example: `dir` means \"list a folder\".\n- An **option** changes *how* the command works. Options are also called **flags** or **switches**.\n- The **target** is *what* to act on, often a folder or file name. It is also called an **argument**.\n\nOptions and targets are often optional. `dir` alone works too.\n\n### Example in CMD\n\nAmina has a folder called `school-notes`. She asks CMD to list it in a **w**ide layout.\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>dir /w school-notes\n Volume in drive C has no label.\n Volume Serial Number is 1A2B-3C4D\n\n Directory of C:\\Users\\Amina\\school-notes\n\n[.]                [..]               [history]          [maths]\n[science]          science-fair.txt   timetable.txt\n               2 File(s)              0 bytes\n               5 Dir(s)  317,988,884,480 bytes free\n```\n\n- Command: `dir`\n- Option: `/w` (wide list)\n- Target: `school-notes`\n\nNames in square brackets, like `[maths]`, are folders. The rest are files. Your serial number and free space will be different.\n\n### The same idea in PowerShell\n\nPowerShell options start with a hyphen and use a whole word. The option `-Name` asks for names only.\n\n```text\nPS C:\\Users\\Amina> dir -Name school-notes\nhistory\nmaths\nscience\nscience-fair.txt\ntimetable.txt\n```\n\n- Command: `dir`\n- Option: `-Name`\n- Target: `school-notes`\n\nCMD has the same \"names only\" idea. It uses the option `/b` (for **b**are).\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>dir /b school-notes\nhistory\nmaths\nscience\nscience-fair.txt\ntimetable.txt\n```\n\n## 🔀 Option Styles Compared\n\nEach terminal writes options in its own style.\n\n| Terminal | Option style | Example | Meaning |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **CMD** | slash `/` | `dir /w` | wide list |\n| **PowerShell** | hyphen + word `-Word` | `dir -Name` | names only |\n| **Git Bash / macOS** | hyphen + letter `-l` | `ls -l` | long, detailed list |\n| **Many modern tools** | two hyphens `--word` | `git --version` | show the version |\n\n> [!TIP]\n> PowerShell does not care about capital letters in commands and options. `dir -name` works the same as `dir -Name`.\n\n## 🗂️ Quotation Marks for Spaces\n\nThe terminal reads a **space** as a gap *between* parts. So a name with spaces confuses it.\n\n**CMD** quietly makes the wrong thing:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>mkdir my new project\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>dir /b\nmy\nnew\nproject\n```\n\nCMD made **three** folders: `my`, `new` and `project`!\n\n**PowerShell** stops with a red error:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice> mkdir my new project\nmkdir : A positional parameter cannot be found that accepts argument 'new'.\n```\n\nThe fix is the same in both. Put **double quotes** around the whole name:\n\n```text\nmkdir \"my new project\"\ncd \"C:\\Program Files\"\n```\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **Best habit**: avoid spaces in names. Use hyphens (`my-new-project`) or underscores (`my_new_project`). Then you never need quotes.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Forgetting the space after the command word.**\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>dir/wschool-notes\nParameter format not correct - \"wschool-notes\".\n```\n\nWithout spaces, the terminal cannot tell where the command ends and the target begins. Put one space between each part:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>dir /w school-notes\n```\n\n**Mixing option styles.** Typing `dir -w` in CMD prints `File Not Found`, because CMD looks for a file called `-w`. In CMD, use `/w`. In PowerShell, use `-Name`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nOpen CMD. Type `dir /w` and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>. Then type `dir /b` and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>. Compare the two lists. Which one is easier to read? Which one fits more names on the screen?\n\n*Hint*: Both commands list the **current** folder, because you gave no target. They only change *how* the list looks.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- The prompt shows where you are standing, like a \"You are here\" dot on a map.\n- PowerShell prompts start with `PS`. Git Bash prompts end with `$`.\n- Commands follow the pattern **command + options + target**.\n- CMD options use `/`, PowerShell options use `-Word`, Git Bash options use `-letter`.\n- Put names with spaces in double quotes, or better, use hyphens instead of spaces.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\n1. Name the three parts of a command.\n2. What does `~` (tilde) mean in a Git Bash prompt?\n3. Why must names with spaces be wrapped in quotes?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. The **command** (the action), the **options** (how to do it) and the **target** (what to act on).\n2. Your **home folder**, for example `C:\\Users\\Amina`.\n3. The terminal treats a space as a gap between parts. Without quotes, one name with spaces becomes several separate names.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nSplit each command into its command, options and target.\n\n1. `dir /s /b C:\\JuniorCoders`\n2. `dir -Name C:\\JuniorCoders`\n3. `mkdir \"School Science Club\"`\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. Command `dir`; options `/s` and `/b`; target `C:\\JuniorCoders`.\n2. Command `dir`; option `-Name`; target `C:\\JuniorCoders`.\n3. Command `mkdir`; no options; target `\"School Science Club\"` (one name, kept together by the quotes).\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA club wants a folder for its website. Two names are suggested: `Junior Coders Club` and `junior-coders-club`.\n\n1. Write the `mkdir` command for each name so that it makes **one** folder.\n2. Which name is better for a website? Give two reasons.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. `mkdir \"Junior Coders Club\"` (quotes needed) and `mkdir junior-coders-club` (no quotes needed).\n2. `junior-coders-club` is better:\n   - In a web address a space turns into `%20`, so the link becomes `Junior%20Coders%20Club`. That is hard to read and type.\n   - You never need quotes, so commands are shorter and safer.\n   - Using all lowercase avoids mix-ups, because many web servers treat `Club` and `club` as different names.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-01-foundations/ch03-where-am-i-and-whats-here.md": "# Chapter 3: Where Am I? & What's Here? (Orientation)\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nImagine you wake up in a new room during a school trip. Before you do anything, you ask two questions. *\"Which room am I in?\"* and *\"What is in this room?\"* Only then do you unpack your bag. Good terminal users do the same. Before they create, move or delete anything, they check where they are and what is around them.\n\n> **Key Idea**: The two most important questions in the terminal are **\"Where am I standing?\"** and **\"What is in this folder?\"** Ask them before every important command.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Print the folder you are standing in, in CMD, PowerShell and Git Bash.\n- List the files and folders in the current folder.\n- Read a `dir` listing: folders, file sizes and the special `.` and `..` entries.\n- Show hidden folders such as `.git`.\n- Clear a crowded screen without losing anything.\n\n## 📍 Where Am I? Printing Your Location\n\nIn File Explorer, you look at the address bar at the top. In the terminal, you ask the computer. The folder you are standing in is called the **working directory**. \"Directory\" is another word for folder.\n\n| Question | CMD | PowerShell |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Where am I? | `cd` (with nothing after it) | `pwd` or `Get-Location` |\n\n`pwd` stands for **p**rint **w**orking **d**irectory.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\simple-site>cd\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\simple-site\n```\n\n**PowerShell**:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\simple-site> pwd\n\nPath\n----\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\simple-site\n```\n\nPowerShell prints a small table with the heading `Path`. Both answers say the same place.\n\n\"But the prompt already shows where I am!\" That is true, most of the time. These commands are useful when the path is very long, or when you need to copy the exact path.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS**: type `pwd`. Git Bash writes Windows paths in its own style:\n> ```text\n> $ pwd\n> /c/JuniorCoders/practice/simple-site\n> ```\n> `C:\\` becomes `/c/`, and backslashes become forward slashes.\n\n## 📋 What Is Here? Listing a Folder\n\nTo see what is inside the current folder, use `dir`. The word comes from **dir**ectory. In PowerShell, `dir` and `ls` both work.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\simple-site>dir\n Volume in drive C has no label.\n Volume Serial Number is 1A2B-3C4D\n\n Directory of C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\simple-site\n\n13-10-2026  08:10    <DIR>          .\n13-10-2026  08:10    <DIR>          ..\n13-10-2026  08:10    <DIR>          css\n13-10-2026  08:10             1,420 index.html\n13-10-2026  08:10    <DIR>          js\n13-10-2026  08:10               850 style.css\n               2 File(s)          2,270 bytes\n               4 Dir(s)  317,987,721,216 bytes free\n```\n\n### Reading the CMD listing\n\n- Each line starts with the **date and time** the item last changed. Your dates will look different.\n- **`<DIR>`** means the item is a folder.\n- A **number** like `1,420` is a file's size in **bytes**. One byte holds about one letter of text.\n- **`.`** (one dot) means *\"this folder\"*.\n- **`..`** (two dots) means *\"the folder one level up\"*. It is called the **parent** folder.\n- The last two lines count the files and folders. They also show the free space on the drive.\n\n**PowerShell**:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\simple-site> ls\n\n    Directory: C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\simple-site\n\nMode                 LastWriteTime         Length Name\n----                 -------------         ------ ----\nd-----        13-10-2026     08:10                css\nd-----        13-10-2026     08:10                js\n-a----        13-10-2026     08:10           1420 index.html\n-a----        13-10-2026     08:10            850 style.css\n```\n\n### Reading the PowerShell listing\n\n- **`Mode`**: a `d` at the start means **d**irectory (folder). A `-` means a file.\n- **`LastWriteTime`**: when the item last changed.\n- **`Length`**: the file size in bytes. Folders have no length.\n- PowerShell lists folders first, then files. It does not show `.` and `..`, but they still work.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS**: type `ls` for a short list, or `ls -l` for a long list with sizes and dates. Git Bash marks folders with a `d` at the start of the line, like PowerShell.\n\n## 🙈 Showing Hidden Items\n\nSome folders are **hidden**. They hold settings that you should not change by accident. A common one is `.git`, made by the Git tool. A normal listing does not show it.\n\n| Show hidden items | CMD | PowerShell | Git Bash / macOS |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Command | `dir /a` | `ls -Force` | `ls -a` |\n\n**PowerShell** with `-Force`:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\simple-site> ls -Force\n\n    Directory: C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\simple-site\n\nMode                 LastWriteTime         Length Name\n----                 -------------         ------ ----\nd--h--        13-10-2026     08:10                .git\nd-----        13-10-2026     08:10                css\nd-----        13-10-2026     08:10                js\n-a----        13-10-2026     08:10           1420 index.html\n-a----        13-10-2026     08:10            850 style.css\n```\n\nThe `h` in `d--h--` means **h**idden. In CMD, `dir /a:h` lists **only** the hidden items:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\simple-site>dir /a:h\n Volume in drive C has no label.\n Volume Serial Number is 1A2B-3C4D\n\n Directory of C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\simple-site\n\n13-10-2026  08:10    <DIR>          .git\n               0 File(s)              0 bytes\n               1 Dir(s)  317,987,713,024 bytes free\n```\n\n## 🧹 Clearing the Screen\n\nAfter many commands, the window gets crowded. You can wipe it clean.\n\n| Clear the screen | CMD | PowerShell | Git Bash / macOS |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Command | `cls` | `cls` or `clear` | `clear` |\n| Shortcut | (none) | <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>L</kbd> | <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>L</kbd> |\n\nThe screen becomes empty, with only a fresh prompt at the top. Clearing does **not** delete files. It does not undo commands either. It is like wiping a whiteboard, not throwing away your notebook.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Typing `pwd` in CMD.** CMD does not know `pwd`.\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>pwd\n'pwd' is not recognized as an internal or external command,\noperable program or batch file.\n```\n\nIn CMD, type `cd` alone. In PowerShell, `pwd` works.\n\n**Typing `cd` alone in PowerShell.** In Windows PowerShell 5.1 (the one built into Windows), `cd` alone prints nothing. In the newer PowerShell 7, it takes you to your home folder! It does not tell you where you are. In PowerShell, always use `pwd`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nMake your practice folder now, using the mouse this time. In Chapter 7 you will learn the terminal way.\n\n1. Open File Explorer. Open the `C:` drive (also called *Local Disk (C:)*).\n2. Make a new folder called `JuniorCoders`. Inside it, make a folder called `practice`.\n3. Open CMD. Type `cd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice` and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>. (You will learn `cd` properly in the next chapter.)\n4. Type `cd` alone. Then type `dir`. What do you see?\n\n*Hint*: A new folder is empty, but `dir` still lists two entries. Which two?\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- The folder you stand in is the **working directory**.\n- CMD: `cd` alone prints it. PowerShell and Git Bash: `pwd`.\n- `dir` lists a folder. PowerShell also accepts `ls`.\n- `<DIR>` (CMD) or a `d` in `Mode` (PowerShell) marks a folder. `.` means this folder, `..` means the parent.\n- `dir /a` (CMD) and `ls -Force` (PowerShell) show hidden items.\n- `cls` or `clear` wipes the screen but never deletes files.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\n1. Which command prints your current folder in CMD? Which one in PowerShell?\n2. What do `.` and `..` mean in a listing?\n3. Which command clears the screen in CMD?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. CMD: `cd` with nothing after it. PowerShell: `pwd` (or `Get-Location`).\n2. `.` is the current folder. `..` is the parent folder, one level up.\n3. `cls`.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nLook at this CMD listing.\n\n```text\n13-10-2026  09:00    <DIR>          .\n13-10-2026  09:00    <DIR>          ..\n13-10-2026  09:02    <DIR>          images\n13-10-2026  09:05             2,048 report.html\n13-10-2026  09:06               512 notes.txt\n               2 File(s)          2,560 bytes\n               3 Dir(s)  100,000,000,000 bytes free\n```\n\n1. How many real folders are inside (not counting `.` and `..`)?\n2. Which is the biggest file, and how big is it?\n3. Write the PowerShell command that lists the same folder.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. One: `images`. (CMD's count of 3 includes `.` and `..`.)\n2. `report.html`, with 2,048 bytes.\n3. `ls` (or `dir`).\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nYusuf's project has a hidden `.git` folder.\n\n1. Why does a plain `dir` or `ls` not show `.git`?\n2. Write the command that shows it in CMD, in PowerShell and in Git Bash.\n3. Why is it a good idea that such folders are hidden?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. The folder has the **hidden** setting. Normal listings skip hidden items.\n2. CMD: `dir /a` (or `dir /a:h` for hidden items only). PowerShell: `ls -Force`. Git Bash: `ls -a`.\n3. `.git` holds Git's own records. If people saw it every day, they might change or delete it by mistake. Hiding it protects the project's history.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-02-navigation-mastery/ch04-moving-between-folders.md": "# Chapter 4: Moving Between Folders (`cd` Mastery)\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nThink of a big school building. To reach the science lab, you walk through the main door, then down a corridor, then into Room 12. To leave, you walk back out the same way, one door at a time. If you get lost, you can always go back to the main entrance. Moving between folders in the terminal works exactly like this.\n\n> **Key Idea**: In File Explorer you double-click a folder to go in, and press the \"Up\" arrow to go out. In the terminal you use `cd`, which means **c**hange **d**irectory.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Step into a folder with `cd` and step out with `cd ..`.\n- Jump several levels up or down in one command.\n- Go straight to the top of a drive, or to your home folder.\n- Switch to another drive, such as a USB flash drive.\n- Read the error message when a folder name is wrong.\n\n## 🚪 Stepping In: `cd folder-name`\n\nTo go into a folder inside your current folder, type `cd`, a space and the folder name. The command prints nothing. Instead, **the prompt changes** to show your new place.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>cd Documents\n\nC:\\Users\\Amina\\Documents>\n```\n\n**PowerShell**:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\Users\\Amina> cd Documents\nPS C:\\Users\\Amina\\Documents>\n```\n\nYou can walk through several folders at once. Put a `\\` between the names:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>cd Documents\\school\\science\n\nC:\\Users\\Amina\\Documents\\school\\science>\n```\n\nThis works the same in PowerShell. PowerShell also accepts forward slashes: `cd Documents/school/science`.\n\n## 🔙 Stepping Out: `cd ..`\n\nRemember from Chapter 3: `..` means *\"the parent folder, one level up\"*. So `cd ..` is the terminal's **Back** button. It is the most used move in the whole terminal.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina\\Documents\\school\\science>cd ..\n\nC:\\Users\\Amina\\Documents\\school>\n```\n\n**PowerShell**:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\Users\\Amina\\Documents\\school\\science> cd ..\nPS C:\\Users\\Amina\\Documents\\school>\n```\n\n### Going up two levels\n\nJoin two `..` with a slash:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina\\Documents\\school\\science>cd ..\\..\n\nC:\\Users\\Amina\\Documents>\n```\n\nIn PowerShell, both `cd ..\\..` and `cd ../..` work.\n\nHere is the path as a ladder. Each `..` climbs one step.\n\n```text\nC:\\\n└── Users\n    └── Amina\n        └── Documents   ◄── after cd ..\\..\n            └── school  ◄── after cd ..\n                └── science  ◄── start\n```\n\n## 🏠 Jumping to the Top or to Home\n\nSometimes you want to go straight to a well-known place.\n\n| Goal | CMD | PowerShell |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Top of the drive (the **root**) | `cd \\` | `cd \\` |\n| Your home folder | `cd %USERPROFILE%` | `cd ~` |\n\nThe **root** is the very top folder of a drive, such as `C:\\`.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina\\Documents\\school>cd \\\n\nC:\\>cd %USERPROFILE%\n\nC:\\Users\\Amina>\n```\n\n`%USERPROFILE%` is a saved setting in Windows. It holds the path of your home folder.\n\n**PowerShell**:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\Users\\Amina\\Documents\\school> cd \\\nPS C:\\> cd ~\nPS C:\\Users\\Amina>\n```\n\n## 💾 Switching Drives\n\nYour computer may have more than one drive. A USB flash drive often appears as `D:` or `E:`.\n\n**CMD** has a surprise. Typing `cd E:\\my-code` while on `C:` does **not** move you to drive E. The prompt stays on `C:`. To switch drives in CMD, type the drive letter and a colon:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>E:\n\nE:\\>cd my-code\n\nE:\\my-code>\n```\n\nOr use the `/d` option, which changes the **d**rive and folder together:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>cd /d E:\\my-code\n\nE:\\my-code>\n```\n\n**PowerShell** is simpler. `cd` changes the drive and the folder at the same time:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\Users\\Amina> cd E:\\my-code\nPS E:\\my-code>\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS**: use forward slashes, as in `cd Documents/school` and `cd ../..`. `cd ~` (or just `cd`) goes home. `cd /` goes to the very top. In Git Bash, drive E is `/e/`, so you type `cd /e/my-code`. A Mac has no drive letters; a USB drive appears under `/Volumes/`.\n\n## 📋 Summary Table\n\n| Goal | CMD | PowerShell | Git Bash / macOS |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Enter a folder | `cd name` | `cd name` | `cd name` |\n| Up one level | `cd ..` | `cd ..` | `cd ..` |\n| Up two levels | `cd ..\\..` | `cd ..\\..` | `cd ../..` |\n| Top of the drive | `cd \\` | `cd \\` | `cd /` |\n| Home folder | `cd %USERPROFILE%` | `cd ~` | `cd ~` |\n| Go to drive E | `E:` or `cd /d E:\\` | `cd E:\\` | `cd /e/` |\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**A small spelling mistake in the folder name.**\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>cd Dekstop\nThe system cannot find the path specified.\n```\n\n**PowerShell** prints a red error. The first line says:\n\n```text\ncd : Cannot find path 'C:\\Users\\Amina\\Dekstop' because it does not exist.\n```\n\nThe terminal is not broken. It just cannot find a folder spelled `Dekstop`. Your position did not change. Fix the spelling:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>cd Desktop\n\nC:\\Users\\Amina\\Desktop>\n```\n\n**Forgetting the space.** `cd..` happens to work in CMD and PowerShell. But PowerShell rejects `cd..\\..` with *\"The term 'cd..\\..' is not recognized\"*. Git Bash rejects even `cd..` with *\"command not found\"*. Always type the space: `cd ..`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nUse the practice folder you made in Chapter 3.\n\n1. Open CMD or PowerShell. You start in your home folder.\n2. Jump to the root with `cd \\`.\n3. Walk down with `cd JuniorCoders`, then `cd practice`.\n4. Climb back to the root in **one** command.\n5. Go home again.\n\n*Hint*: From `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice`, you need to climb two levels. Then use the home command from the table.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- `cd name` steps into a folder. The prompt changes to show where you are.\n- `cd ..` steps out one level. `cd ..\\..` steps out two.\n- `cd \\` jumps to the top of the drive. `cd ~` (PowerShell) or `cd %USERPROFILE%` (CMD) goes home.\n- In CMD, switch drives with `E:` or `cd /d E:\\folder`. In PowerShell, `cd E:\\folder` is enough.\n- A wrong name gives an error but changes nothing. Check the spelling and try again.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\n1. What does `cd` stand for?\n2. What does `..` mean?\n3. How do you jump to the root of the current drive?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. **C**hange **d**irectory.\n2. The parent folder, one level up.\n3. `cd \\` in CMD and PowerShell (`cd /` in Git Bash).\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nYour prompt shows `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\lessons\\day-01>`.\n\n1. Write the command to move up to `lessons`.\n2. Write one command to move up to `C:\\JuniorCoders`.\n3. Inside `practice` there is also a folder called `assets`. Write one command to go from `day-01` straight into `assets`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `cd ..`\n2. `cd ..\\..\\..` (three steps: `lessons`, then `practice`, then `JuniorCoders`).\n3. `cd ..\\..\\assets` (up to `lessons`, up to `practice`, then down into `assets`).\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nZainab is in CMD at `C:\\Users\\Zainab>`. She types `cd E:\\science-fair` to open her flash drive. She says: *\"Nothing happened! It still says `C:\\Users\\Zainab>`.\"*\n\n1. Explain what happened.\n2. Show her **two** ways to fix it in CMD.\n3. What would happen if she typed the same command in PowerShell?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. In CMD, `cd` changes the folder **on drive E** in the background, but it does not switch the window to drive E. She is still on `C:`.\n2. Way one: type `E:` and press Enter. She arrives at `E:\\science-fair`, because CMD remembered that folder for drive E. Way two: type `cd /d E:\\science-fair`.\n3. In PowerShell, `cd E:\\science-fair` switches the drive and the folder together. The prompt would show `PS E:\\science-fair>`.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-02-navigation-mastery/ch05-paths-relative-vs-absolute.md": "# Chapter 5: Relative vs. Absolute Paths\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nImagine you invite a friend to your home. You can give the **full address**: *\"House 4, Lal Chowk, Srinagar, Jammu and Kashmir, India.\"* Anyone, anywhere, can find it. Or you can give **directions from where they stand**: *\"Leave the bakery, turn right, it is the second house.\"* That only works if your friend is already at the bakery. Paths in a computer come in the same two kinds.\n\n> **Key Idea**: A **path** is the address of a file or folder. An **absolute path** is the full address, starting from the top of the drive. A **relative path** gives directions starting from the folder you are standing in.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Tell an absolute path from a relative path at a glance.\n- Use `.`, `..` and the folder separator inside paths.\n- Reach the same folder with both kinds of path in CMD and PowerShell.\n- Explain why websites must use relative paths for their own files.\n\n## 🗺️ The Map We Will Use\n\nHere is a small website inside the practice folder. We will use it for every example.\n\n```text\nC:\\\n└── JuniorCoders\n    └── practice\n        └── my-website   ◄── you are here\n            ├── index.html\n            ├── css\n            │   └── style.css\n            └── js\n                └── app.js\n```\n\n## 📮 Absolute Paths: The Full Address\n\nAn **absolute path** starts at the **root** of a drive. On Windows it begins with a drive letter, like `C:\\`. It works from **anywhere**, because it gives the whole address.\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-website\\css\\style.css\n```\n\nLet us list the `css` folder using its absolute path, from a completely different place.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>dir /b C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-website\\css\nstyle.css\n```\n\n**PowerShell**:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\Users\\Amina> dir -Name C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-website\\css\nstyle.css\n```\n\nAmina was standing in her home folder. The full address still worked.\n\n## 🧭 Relative Paths: Directions From Here\n\nA **relative path** does **not** start with a drive letter or `\\`. The terminal reads it starting from your **current folder**.\n\nNow Amina moves into `my-website` first.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>cd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-website\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-website>dir /b css\nstyle.css\n```\n\n**PowerShell**:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\Users\\Amina> cd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-website\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-website> dir -Name css\nstyle.css\n```\n\n`css` means *\"the css folder right here\"*. It is short and easy to type.\n\n### Climbing with `..`\n\nFrom inside the `css` folder, how do you list the `js` folder? Go **up** one level, then **down** into `js`:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-website\\css>dir /b ..\\js\napp.js\n```\n\nIn PowerShell the command is `dir -Name ..\\js`, with the same answer: `app.js`.\n\n## 📊 Comparing Both Kinds\n\nStanding in `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-website`:\n\n| Item | Absolute path | Relative path |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `index.html` | `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-website\\index.html` | `index.html` or `.\\index.html` |\n| `css` folder | `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-website\\css` | `css` |\n| `style.css` | `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-website\\css\\style.css` | `css\\style.css` |\n| `practice` folder | `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice` | `..` |\n\n## 🔣 Path Symbols\n\n| Symbol | Meaning | Example |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `.` (one dot) | this folder | `.\\index.html` |\n| `..` (two dots) | the parent folder | `..\\js\\app.js` |\n| `\\` at the start | the root of this drive | `\\JuniorCoders` |\n| `\\` in the middle | a separator between names | `css\\style.css` |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS** use forward slashes: `css/style.css`, `../js/app.js`, `./index.html`. An absolute path starts with `/`, such as `/c/JuniorCoders/practice` in Git Bash. PowerShell happily accepts forward slashes too.\n\n## 🌐 Why Web Developers Care\n\nA web page connects to its other files with paths. Here is how `index.html` might load its style file and script:\n\n```html\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"css/style.css\">\n<script src=\"js/app.js\"></script>\n```\n\nThese are **relative** paths. They mean *\"start from the folder where this page is\"*.\n\nNow imagine you wrote an absolute path instead:\n\n```html\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-website\\css\\style.css\">\n```\n\nIt works on your computer only. When the site goes onto a web server, that server has no `C:\\JuniorCoders` folder. The link breaks, and the page loses all its styles.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **Golden rule for websites**: use relative paths to link your own pages, styles, scripts and images. Then the site works on any computer and any server.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Using a relative path from the wrong place.** Amina is in her home folder and types:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>cd css\nThe system cannot find the path specified.\n```\n\nThere is no `css` folder **inside her home folder**. A relative path always starts from where you are. Two fixes:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>cd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-website\\css\n```\n\nor first walk to `my-website`, then type `cd css`.\n\n**Starting a relative path with `\\`.** `cd \\css` means *\"the css folder at the top of the drive\"*, which does not exist. Leave out the first `\\` for a relative path.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\n1. Go to `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice` using its **absolute** path.\n2. Go up to `C:\\JuniorCoders` using a **relative** path.\n3. Go back into `practice` using a relative path.\n4. Check where you are with `cd` (CMD) or `pwd` (PowerShell).\n\n*Hint*: An absolute path starts with `C:\\`. A relative path to the parent is just two dots.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- A **path** is an address for a file or folder.\n- An **absolute path** starts from the root (`C:\\`) and works from anywhere.\n- A **relative path** starts from your current folder. It is shorter, but depends on where you stand.\n- `.` means this folder and `..` means the parent folder.\n- Websites must use relative paths for their own files, so they work on every server.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\n1. What is the difference between an absolute path and a relative path?\n2. Which kind of path starts with a drive letter like `C:\\`?\n3. What does a single `.` mean in a path?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. An absolute path gives the full address from the root of the drive. A relative path gives directions from the current folder.\n2. An absolute path.\n3. The current folder (\"this folder\").\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nHere is a project:\n\n```text\nschool-portal\n├── index.html\n├── pages\n│   └── about.html\n└── assets\n    └── logo.png\n```\n\n1. The file `about.html` wants to show `logo.png`. What relative path should it use?\n2. What relative path takes `about.html` back to `index.html`?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `../assets/logo.png` (up from `pages` to `school-portal`, then into `assets`). Web pages use forward slashes.\n2. `../index.html`\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA classmate wrote this in a web page:\n\n```html\n<img src=\"C:\\Users\\Admin\\Desktop\\school-site\\images\\banner.jpg\" alt=\"School banner\">\n```\n\nThe page is saved as `C:\\Users\\Admin\\Desktop\\school-site\\index.html`.\n\n1. Explain why the picture disappears once the site is on a web server.\n2. Rewrite the tag with a correct relative path.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. The path points to a folder on the classmate's own computer. The web server has no `C:\\Users\\Admin\\Desktop` folder, and visitors' browsers cannot reach that computer. So the browser cannot find the picture.\n2. The page and the `images` folder are both inside `school-site`, so:\n\n```html\n<img src=\"images/banner.jpg\" alt=\"School banner\">\n```\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-02-navigation-mastery/ch06-tab-completion-and-history.md": "# Chapter 6: The Developer Superpowers: Tab Completion & History\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nHafsa is writing a report in the school library. When she starts typing a long word on her tablet, the keyboard guesses it, and she taps the guess. When she needs a sentence she wrote earlier, she scrolls back instead of writing it again. The terminal has both of these helpers. They save time, and they stop spelling mistakes before they happen.\n\n> **Key Idea**: Good terminal users rarely type long names in full. They press <kbd>Tab</kbd> to finish names, and the arrow keys to bring back earlier commands.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Finish file and folder names with <kbd>Tab</kbd> in CMD, PowerShell and Git Bash.\n- Use <kbd>Tab</kbd> as a quick check that a name really exists.\n- Bring back earlier commands with the <kbd>↑</kbd> and <kbd>↓</kbd> arrow keys.\n- Show your command history as a list.\n- Use the most helpful keyboard shortcuts, including the <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd> \"stop\" key.\n\n## ⌨️ Superpower 1: Tab Completion\n\n**Tab completion** means the terminal finishes a name for you. You type the first few letters, then press the <kbd>Tab</kbd> key.\n\nSuppose your practice folder holds a folder called `science-fair-2026-project`. That is 25 letters. Here is the quick way.\n\n**CMD**: type `cd sci`, then press <kbd>Tab</kbd>.\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>cd sci          ← what you type\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>cd science-fair-2026-project   ← after Tab\n```\n\n**PowerShell**: type `cd sci`, then press <kbd>Tab</kbd>.\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice> cd sci       ← what you type\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice> cd .\\science-fair-2026-project\\   ← after Tab\n```\n\nPowerShell adds `.\\` at the start (\"in this folder\") and `\\` at the end (\"it is a folder\"). Both are correct. Press <kbd>Enter</kbd> to run the command.\n\nIf the name has a space, Tab adds the quotes for you: `cd \"my notes\"`.\n\n### When several names match\n\nSuppose you have `day-01`, `day-02` and `day-03`.\n\n- **CMD and PowerShell**: press <kbd>Tab</kbd> again and again. Each press shows the next match. <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>Tab</kbd> goes back.\n- **PowerShell extra**: press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Space</kbd> to see **all** matches in a small menu. Pick one with the arrow keys.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS**: one <kbd>Tab</kbd> finishes the name when there is only one match. If there are several, press <kbd>Tab</kbd> **twice** to list them all. Then type one more letter and press <kbd>Tab</kbd> again.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Tab is a spelling check.** If you press <kbd>Tab</kbd> and nothing changes, no file or folder starts with those letters **here**. Check your spelling, or check where you are standing.\n\n## 🕰️ Superpower 2: Command History\n\nThe terminal remembers the commands you ran in this window. This list is called the **history**.\n\n| Key or command | CMD | PowerShell | Git Bash / macOS |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Previous command | <kbd>↑</kbd> | <kbd>↑</kbd> | <kbd>↑</kbd> |\n| Next command | <kbd>↓</kbd> | <kbd>↓</kbd> | <kbd>↓</kbd> |\n| Pop-up list | <kbd>F7</kbd> | (none) | (none) |\n| Print the list | `doskey /history` | `history` | `history` |\n| Search the history | (none) | <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>R</kbd> | <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>R</kbd> |\n\nPressing <kbd>↑</kbd> puts the last command back on the line. It does **not** run it yet. You can change it first, then press <kbd>Enter</kbd>.\n\n### Printing the history\n\nAmina ran three commands: `cd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice`, `dir` and `cls`. Now she prints her history.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>doskey /history\ncd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\ndir\ncls\ndoskey /history\n```\n\n**PowerShell**:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice> history\n\n  Id CommandLine\n  -- -----------\n   1 cd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\n   2 dir\n   3 cls\n```\n\nEach window keeps its own history. In CMD, closing the window forgets it. PowerShell keeps a copy, so <kbd>↑</kbd> can bring back commands even after you reopen it.\n\n### Why this matters\n\nIn Chapter 11 you will start a small web server with a long command. You will stop it, change your page, and start it again many times. Instead of retyping, you will press <kbd>↑</kbd> and <kbd>Enter</kbd>. Two key presses!\n\n## ⚡ Helpful Keyboard Shortcuts\n\n| Shortcut | What it does | Works in |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd> | **Stop.** Cancels the line you are typing, or stops a running command | CMD, PowerShell, Git Bash |\n| <kbd>Esc</kbd> | Wipes the line you are typing, without running it | CMD, PowerShell |\n| <kbd>Home</kbd> / <kbd>End</kbd> | Jumps to the start / end of the line | CMD, PowerShell, Git Bash |\n| <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>A</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>E</kbd> | Jumps to the start / end of the line | Git Bash, macOS |\n| <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>L</kbd> | Clears the screen | PowerShell, Git Bash |\n\nWhen you press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd> on a half-typed line in CMD, you see `^C` and a fresh prompt. Nothing was run.\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>dir /w scie^C\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>\n```\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Pressing Tab in the wrong folder.** Bilal is in his home folder. He types `cd sci` and presses <kbd>Tab</kbd>. Nothing happens. He thinks Tab is broken.\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Bilal>cd sci\n```\n\nTab only looks in the **current** folder, or in the folder your path points to. The folder is in `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice`. The fix is to go there first, or type the path and then press Tab:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Bilal>cd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\sci          ← then press Tab\nC:\\Users\\Bilal>cd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\science-fair-2026-project\n```\n\n**Pressing Enter too fast after ↑.** The arrow brings back an old command, and a quick <kbd>Enter</kbd> runs it again. Always read the line before you press Enter, especially if it changes files.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\n1. In File Explorer, make a folder called `science-fair-2026-project` inside `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice`.\n2. In the terminal, go to `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice`.\n3. Type `cd sci` and press <kbd>Tab</kbd>, then <kbd>Enter</kbd>.\n4. Run `cd ..`, then `dir`, then `cls`.\n5. Press <kbd>↑</kbd> three times. Watch the commands come back in reverse order.\n6. Print your history (`doskey /history` in CMD, `history` in PowerShell).\n\n*Hint*: If Tab does not finish the name, check your spelling and your current folder.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- <kbd>Tab</kbd> finishes file and folder names. Press it again to see other matches.\n- If Tab does nothing, the name does not exist where you are looking.\n- <kbd>↑</kbd> and <kbd>↓</kbd> bring back earlier commands. Read them before you press Enter.\n- `doskey /history` (CMD) and `history` (PowerShell, Git Bash) print your history.\n- <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd> is the stop key. <kbd>Esc</kbd> wipes the line in CMD and PowerShell.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\n1. Which key finishes a half-typed file or folder name?\n2. How do you bring back the last command without typing it again?\n3. Which key combination stops a running command?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. <kbd>Tab</kbd>.\n2. Press the <kbd>↑</kbd> (Up arrow) key.\n3. <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd>.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nA folder contains `report-maths.txt`, `report-science.txt` and `reading-list.txt`.\n\n1. You type `dir rep` in CMD and press <kbd>Tab</kbd>. Which name appears first?\n2. What do you press to see the next match?\n3. What is the shortest start you could type so that one <kbd>Tab</kbd> finishes `reading-list.txt` straight away?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `report-maths.txt` (matches come in alphabetical order).\n2. <kbd>Tab</kbd> again (or <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>Tab</kbd> to go back).\n3. `rea`. Both `rep...` and `rea...` start with `re`, so you need the third letter.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nWeb servers often run on Linux, where `Report.txt` and `report.txt` are two **different** files. Capital letters matter there.\n\n1. How does Tab completion help you avoid mistakes with capital letters?\n2. How does it help with long paths like `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\science-fair-2026-project\\results`?\n3. Write a short rule for your class about using Tab.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. Tab copies the real name exactly, with the right capitals. You cannot misspell it.\n2. You type only a few letters of each folder and press Tab each time. Every part is checked as you go. If one part is wrong, Tab stops working right there, so you find the mistake early.\n3. *\"Type three letters, press Tab. If Tab does nothing, stop and check: is the name right, and am I in the right folder?\"*\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-03-managing-files-and-folders/ch07-creating-folders-and-files.md": "# Chapter 7: Creating Folders & Files at Lightning Speed\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nAt the start of term, Maryam's teacher asks her to prepare files for the class library project. In File Explorer, each new folder needs a right-click, \"New\", \"Folder\", and then typing a name. Ten folders mean forty clicks. Her older brother shows her the terminal. He types one short line and presses Enter. All ten folders appear at once, with neat, correct names.\n\n> **Key Idea**: In the terminal you can create several folders and files with one line. It is fast, exact and easy to repeat.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Make your practice folder with `mkdir`.\n- Create one folder, several folders, and folders inside folders.\n- Create an empty file in CMD and in PowerShell.\n- Create a small text file with one line inside it.\n- Build a complete website skeleton in a few lines.\n\n## 📁 Creating a Folder: `mkdir`\n\n`mkdir` stands for **m**a**k**e **dir**ectory. It works in CMD, PowerShell and Git Bash.\n\nLet us make the practice folder the terminal way. If you already made it in Chapter 3, you will see a friendly \"already exists\" message. That is fine.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>mkdir C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\n\nC:\\Users\\Amina>cd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>\n```\n\nCMD prints nothing when it succeeds. Silence means success.\n\n**PowerShell**:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\Users\\Amina> mkdir C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\n\n    Directory: C:\\JuniorCoders\n\nMode                 LastWriteTime         Length Name\n----                 -------------         ------ ----\nd-----        13-10-2026     08:14                practice\n```\n\nPowerShell shows a small table of what it made. The `d` in `Mode` tells you it is a folder.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **From now on, every exercise happens inside `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice`.** It is your safe practice space. Nothing important lives there.\n\n## 📂 Several Folders at Once\n\nHere CMD and PowerShell are different.\n\n**CMD**: separate the names with **spaces**.\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>mkdir css js images\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>dir /b\ncss\nimages\njs\n```\n\n**PowerShell**: separate the names with **commas**.\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice> mkdir css, js, images\n\n    Directory: C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\n\nMode                 LastWriteTime         Length Name\n----                 -------------         ------ ----\nd-----        13-10-2026     08:14                css\nd-----        13-10-2026     08:14                js\nd-----        13-10-2026     08:14                images\n```\n\n## 🪆 Folders Inside Folders\n\nYou can make a folder **and** the folders around it in one go. CMD and PowerShell both create any missing folders along the path.\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>mkdir assets\\images\\icons\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>cd assets\\images\\icons\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\assets\\images\\icons>\n```\n\nThe same `mkdir assets\\images\\icons` works in PowerShell.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS**: separate names with spaces: `mkdir css js images`. For folders inside folders, add `-p` (for **p**arents): `mkdir -p assets/images/icons`. Without `-p`, Git Bash stops with \"No such file or directory\".\n\n## 📄 Creating an Empty File\n\nAn **empty file** has a name but nothing inside (0 bytes). Developers often make empty files first and fill them later.\n\n| Task | CMD | PowerShell |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Make an empty file | `type nul > index.html` | `New-Item index.html` |\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>type nul > index.html\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>\n```\n\nHere is how it works:\n- `nul` is a special Windows \"nothing\" device. Reading it gives no text at all.\n- `>` is the **redirect** sign. It sends the result into a file instead of the screen.\n- So `type nul > index.html` means *\"put nothing into index.html\"*. You get a new, empty file.\n\n**PowerShell**:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice> New-Item index.html\n\n    Directory: C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\n\nMode                 LastWriteTime         Length Name\n----                 -------------         ------ ----\n-a----        13-10-2026     08:14              0 index.html\n```\n\nThe `Length` is `0`, so the file is empty. A short name for `New-Item` is `ni`.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS**: `touch index.html` makes an empty file. If the file already exists, `touch` only updates its time. It never empties it.\n\n## 📝 A File With One Line Inside\n\n`echo` prints text. With `>` you can send that text into a new file.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>echo Science fair notes > notes.txt\n```\n\n**PowerShell** (put the text in quotes):\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice> echo \"Science fair notes\" > notes.txt\n```\n\nTo open any file in Notepad and type more, use `notepad notes.txt`. This works in both terminals. You will learn to read files in the terminal in Chapter 12.\n\n## 🏗️ Drill: A Website Skeleton in Six Lines\n\nWatch a whole website skeleton appear.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nmkdir portfolio\ncd portfolio\nmkdir css js images\ntype nul > index.html\ntype nul > css\\style.css\ntype nul > js\\app.js\n```\n\n**PowerShell**:\n\n```text\nmkdir portfolio\ncd portfolio\nmkdir css, js, images\nNew-Item index.html\nNew-Item css\\style.css\nNew-Item js\\app.js\n```\n\nCheck your work with `dir /s /b` (CMD). It lists every folder and file inside, at every level:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\portfolio>dir /s /b\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\portfolio\\css\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\portfolio\\images\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\portfolio\\index.html\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\portfolio\\js\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\portfolio\\css\\style.css\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\portfolio\\js\\app.js\n```\n\nIn PowerShell, type `ls -Recurse -Name`:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\portfolio> ls -Recurse -Name\ncss\nimages\njs\nindex.html\ncss\\style.css\njs\\app.js\n```\n\nThat would take about twenty clicks in File Explorer.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Using spaces between names in PowerShell.**\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice> mkdir css js images\nmkdir : A positional parameter cannot be found that accepts argument 'js'.\n```\n\nPowerShell made nothing. Use commas: `mkdir css, js, images`.\n\n**Using `type nul >` on a file that already has work in it.** The `>` sign **replaces** everything in the file.\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>type nul > notes.txt\n```\n\nCMD gives no warning. Your notes are now gone, and the file is empty. Before you create a file, list the folder with `dir` to check the name is free. (PowerShell's `New-Item` is safer: it refuses with *\"The file ... already exists.\"*)\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nInside `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice`, build this for a class charity drive:\n\n```text\ncharity-drive\n├── index.html\n├── donors.txt\n└── pages\n    └── about.html\n```\n\n*Hint*: Make `charity-drive` and go in. Make the `pages` folder. Then make the three empty files. Finish with `dir /s /b` or `ls -Recurse -Name` to check.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- `mkdir name` makes a folder. Silence in CMD means it worked.\n- Several folders: spaces in CMD (`mkdir a b c`), commas in PowerShell (`mkdir a, b, c`).\n- `mkdir a\\b\\c` makes all the missing folders on the way, in both terminals.\n- Empty file: `type nul > name` (CMD) or `New-Item name` (PowerShell).\n- `>` replaces a file's contents, so check with `dir` before using it.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\n1. Which command makes a new folder in CMD and PowerShell?\n2. Write one command that makes folders `alpha`, `beta` and `gamma` in CMD. Then write it for PowerShell.\n3. How do you make an empty `index.html` in CMD? And in PowerShell?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `mkdir`.\n2. CMD: `mkdir alpha beta gamma`. PowerShell: `mkdir alpha, beta, gamma`.\n3. CMD: `type nul > index.html`. PowerShell: `New-Item index.html`.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nIn your practice folder:\n\n1. Make a folder called `practice-lab` and go into it.\n2. In one line, make the folders `pages` and `assets`.\n3. Make an empty file called `index.html`.\n4. List the folder to check.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nCMD:\n\n```text\nmkdir practice-lab\ncd practice-lab\nmkdir pages assets\ntype nul > index.html\ndir /b\n```\n\nPowerShell:\n\n```text\nmkdir practice-lab\ncd practice-lab\nmkdir pages, assets\nNew-Item index.html\ndir -Name\n```\n\nThe list shows `assets`, `index.html` and `pages`.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nLook at this CMD command:\n\n```text\necho Welcome to the reading club > readme.txt\n```\n\n1. What does the `>` sign do here?\n2. `readme.txt` already holds a long list of book titles. What happens to that list?\n3. Write a safe habit that protects you from this mistake.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. `>` sends the text from `echo` into the file `readme.txt` instead of the screen.\n2. The list is **replaced**. The file now holds only `Welcome to the reading club`. There is no warning and no Recycle Bin copy.\n3. List the folder first (`dir`) and check the name is not already used. If it is, choose a new name or make a copy first. (Chapter 12 shows `>>`, which adds to the end instead of replacing.)\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-03-managing-files-and-folders/ch08-renaming-moving-and-copying.md": "# Chapter 8: Renaming, Moving & Copying Files\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nIbrahim helps in the school library after classes. Sometimes a book has the wrong label, so he writes a new one: that is **renaming**. Sometimes a book sits on the wrong shelf, so he carries it to the right one: that is **moving**. When a teacher needs a worksheet for every student, he photocopies it: that is **copying**. The original stays safe. The terminal has a command for each of these jobs.\n\n> **Key Idea**: Renaming, moving and copying from the terminal is exact. You never drop a file into the wrong folder by accident, as can happen when dragging with a mouse.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Rename files and folders with `ren`.\n- Move files into another folder, or up to the parent folder, with `move`.\n- Copy files, and whole folders, with `copy` (and `xcopy` in CMD).\n- Spot the important differences between CMD and PowerShell, especially about overwriting.\n\nAll examples happen in `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site`. Make it now and add some files to follow along:\n\n```text\nmkdir C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site\ncd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site\nmkdir css js\n```\n\nThen make the empty files `styles.txt`, `script.js` and `page.html` (Chapter 7 shows how).\n\n## 🏷️ Renaming: `ren`\n\n`ren` is short for **ren**ame. Pattern: `ren old-name new-name`.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site>ren styles.txt style.css\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site>\n```\n\n**PowerShell**:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site> ren styles.txt style.css\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site>\n```\n\nBoth print nothing when they succeed. Run `dir` to see the new name. Folders are renamed the same way: `ren old-folder new-folder`.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS** have no rename command. They use `mv` (move) for both jobs: `mv styles.txt style.css`.\n\n## 🚚 Moving: `move`\n\nMoving takes a file out of one folder and puts it in another. Pattern: `move file destination`.\n\n**CMD** tells you how many files moved:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site>move script.js js\\\n        1 file(s) moved.\n```\n\n**PowerShell** says nothing when it works:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site> move script.js js\\\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site>\n```\n\nThe `\\` after `js` says *\"js is a folder\"*. It is a good habit, as you will see below.\n\n### More ways to move\n\n| Task | CMD and PowerShell | Git Bash / macOS |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Into a subfolder | `move script.js js\\` | `mv script.js js/` |\n| Up to the parent folder | `move logo.png ..` | `mv logo.png ..` |\n| Move and rename together | `move draft.txt js\\notes.txt` | `mv draft.txt js/notes.txt` |\n\n## 📑 Copying: `copy`\n\nCopying makes a second file. The original stays where it was. Pattern: `copy original new-copy`.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site>copy page.html backup.html\n        1 file(s) copied.\n```\n\n**PowerShell** is silent again:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site> copy page.html backup.html\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site>\n```\n\nTo copy into a folder, give the folder as the destination: `copy page.html css\\`.\n\n### A safe backup before a big change\n\nBefore you try a big change to your style file, make a backup copy:\n\n```text\ncopy style.css style.backup.css\n```\n\nNow you can experiment without fear. If things go wrong, the backup is still there.\n\n## 🗃️ Copying a Whole Folder\n\n`copy` in CMD only copies files. For a folder with everything inside, CMD uses `xcopy`. The options are `/e` (include **e**very subfolder, even empty ones) and `/i` (the destination **i**s a folder).\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>xcopy library-site library-site-backup /e /i\nlibrary-site\\backup.html\nlibrary-site\\page.html\nlibrary-site\\style.css\nlibrary-site\\js\\script.js\n4 File(s) copied\n```\n\n**PowerShell** uses `copy` with `-Recurse`, which means \"go into every subfolder too\":\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice> copy library-site library-site-backup -Recurse\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS**: `cp page.html backup.html` copies a file. `cp -r library-site library-site-backup` copies a whole folder (`-r` means recursive, \"go into every subfolder\").\n\n## 📋 Summary Table\n\n| Task | CMD | PowerShell | Git Bash / macOS |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Rename | `ren a.js b.js` | `ren a.js b.js` | `mv a.js b.js` |\n| Move into folder | `move a.js js\\` | `move a.js js\\` | `mv a.js js/` |\n| Move up one level | `move a.js ..` | `move a.js ..` | `mv a.js ..` |\n| Copy a file | `copy a.txt b.txt` | `copy a.txt b.txt` | `cp a.txt b.txt` |\n| Copy a folder | `xcopy a b /e /i` | `copy a b -Recurse` | `cp -r a b` |\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**PowerShell copies over a file without asking.** Bilal already has `backup.html` with yesterday's good work. He types:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site> copy page.html backup.html\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site>\n```\n\nNo warning! The old `backup.html` is replaced. In **CMD**, typed at the prompt, `copy` stops and asks first:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site>copy page.html backup.html\nOverwrite backup.html? (Yes/No/All):\n```\n\nType `N` and press Enter to keep the old file. In PowerShell, always run `dir` first to check the new name is free.\n\n**Moving into a folder that does not exist.** Suppose there is **no** `scripts` folder, and you type `move app.js scripts`. The file is not moved. It is **renamed** to a file called `scripts`, with no extension! Add the `\\` to be safe:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site>move app.js scripts\\\nThe system cannot find the path specified.\n        0 file(s) moved.\n```\n\nThe error tells you the folder is missing, and nothing changes. Make the folder with `mkdir scripts`, then move again.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nIn `library-site`:\n\n1. Make a file called `catalog-draft.txt`.\n2. Rename it to `catalog.txt`.\n3. Make a folder called `data` and move `catalog.txt` into it.\n4. Copy it back to the main folder as `catalog-copy.txt`.\n5. Check with `dir /s /b` (CMD) or `ls -Recurse -Name` (PowerShell).\n\n*Hint*: For step 4, stand in `library-site` and type `copy data\\catalog.txt catalog-copy.txt`.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- `ren old new` renames a file or folder.\n- `move file folder\\` moves a file. `move file ..` moves it up one level.\n- `copy original copy` duplicates a file. Use `xcopy /e /i` (CMD) or `copy -Recurse` (PowerShell) for folders.\n- CMD prints how many files moved or copied. PowerShell stays silent when it works.\n- PowerShell's `copy` overwrites without asking. Check with `dir` first, and end folder names with `\\`.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\n1. Which command renames a file in CMD and PowerShell? Which one does Git Bash use?\n2. How do you move `banner.png` up into the parent folder?\n3. What is the difference between `copy` and `move`?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `ren` in CMD and PowerShell. Git Bash uses `mv`.\n2. `move banner.png ..`\n3. `copy` makes a second file and leaves the original where it was. `move` takes the file away from its old place.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nYour folder has `main.css` and `index.html` in it, and an empty `css` folder.\n\n1. Move `main.css` into `css`.\n2. Make a backup copy of `index.html` called `index.backup.html`.\n3. Rename `index.backup.html` to `home-backup.html`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```text\nmove main.css css\\\ncopy index.html index.backup.html\nren index.backup.html home-backup.html\n```\n\nThese three lines work in both CMD and PowerShell.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nAisha keeps her class newsletter in `newsletter.html`. Her helper wants to save a copy called `newsletter-old.html`, but a file with that name already holds last month's issue.\n\n1. What happens if the helper runs `copy newsletter.html newsletter-old.html` in CMD? And in PowerShell?\n2. Why does the CMD question protect people's work?\n3. Write three commands that keep **both** old files safe and still make the new backup.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. CMD asks `Overwrite newsletter-old.html? (Yes/No/All):` and waits. PowerShell replaces the file at once, without asking.\n2. It gives a moment to stop and think. Last month's issue is someone's work, and keeping it safe is part of our *Amānah* (trust).\n3. For example:\n\n```text\ndir\nren newsletter-old.html newsletter-september.html\ncopy newsletter.html newsletter-old.html\n```\n\nFirst list the folder, then give last month's file a clear new name, then make the new copy.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-03-managing-files-and-folders/ch09-safe-deletion-and-amanah.md": "# Chapter 9: Safe Deletion & Digital Trust (*Amānah*)\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nIn the school kitchen garden, Yusuf is asked to pull out the weeds. A careless helper pulls quickly and also pulls out the new bean plants. Yusuf works differently. He looks at each plant first. He checks it really is a weed. Then he pulls out just that one. Deleting files from the terminal needs the same care, because there is no way to put the plant back.\n\n> **Key Idea**: Terminal deletions **skip the Recycle Bin**. The file is gone at once. So we always look first, delete one named item at a time, and work only inside our practice folder.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Delete a single file with `del` in CMD and PowerShell.\n- Ask the terminal to confirm before it deletes.\n- Remove empty folders, and folders with files, safely.\n- Follow the three golden rules of safe deletion.\n- Explain why careful deletion is part of *Amānah* (trust).\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> **No Recycle Bin. No undo.** Practise every command in this chapter **only** inside `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice`. Never on the desktop, never in Documents, never on a shared computer's folders.\n\n## 🔍 Golden Rule 1: Look Before You Delete\n\nBefore every deletion, check **where** you are and **what** is there.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>cd\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>dir /b\njunk.txt\nnotes.txt\nold-drafts\n```\n\n**PowerShell**:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice> dir -Name\nold-drafts\njunk.txt\nnotes.txt\n```\n\nNow you know exactly which items exist. You are ready to choose one.\n\n## 🗑️ Deleting One File: `del`\n\n`del` is short for **del**ete. Type it with **one exact file name**.\n\n**CMD** (it prints nothing when it works):\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>del junk.txt\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>dir /b\nnotes.txt\nold-drafts\n```\n\n**PowerShell** (also silent):\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice> del junk.txt\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice> dir -Name\nold-drafts\nnotes.txt\n```\n\nIf the file is not there, CMD says so, and nothing else happens:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>del junk.txt\nCould Not Find C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\junk.txt\n```\n\n### Golden Rule 2: Ask for a \"Are you sure?\"\n\nBoth terminals can ask you before they delete.\n\n**CMD** uses `/p` (for **p**rompt):\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>del /p notes.txt\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\notes.txt, Delete (Y/N)? N\n```\n\n**PowerShell** uses `-Confirm`:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice> del notes.txt -Confirm\n\nConfirm\nAre you sure you want to perform this action?\nPerforming the operation \"Remove File\" on target \"C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\notes.txt\".\n[Y] Yes  [A] Yes to All  [N] No  [L] No to All  [S] Suspend  [?] Help (default is \"Y\"):\n```\n\nType `N` and press Enter to keep the file. Type `Y` to delete it.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS**: `rm junk.txt` deletes a file. `rm -i junk.txt` asks first (`-i` means **i**nteractive). Like Windows, `rm` skips the bin.\n\n## 📁 Deleting Folders\n\n### An empty folder: `rmdir`\n\n`rmdir` means **r**e**m**ove **dir**ectory. It only removes **empty** folders. That makes it safe.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>rmdir old-drafts\nThe directory is not empty.\n```\n\nCMD refused, because `old-drafts` still has files. Good! It protected you. Look inside first:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>dir /b old-drafts\ndraft-1.txt\n```\n\n### A folder with files inside\n\nIf you have checked and you really want the folder and everything in it gone, use `rmdir /s`. The `/s` means *\"with all its **s**ubfolders and files\"*. CMD then asks you to be sure:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>rmdir /s old-drafts\nold-drafts, Are you sure (Y/N)? Y\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>\n```\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> You may see `/q` (quiet) in other guides. It **removes this question**. We do not use it. The question is your last chance to stop.\n\n**PowerShell**: `rmdir old-drafts` works on an empty folder. If the folder has files, PowerShell stops and asks:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice> rmdir old-drafts\n\nConfirm\nThe item at C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\old-drafts has children and the Recurse parameter was not specified. If you\ncontinue, all children will be removed with the item. Are you sure you want to continue?\n[Y] Yes  [A] Yes to All  [N] No  [L] No to All  [S] Suspend  [?] Help (default is \"Y\"):\n```\n\n\"Children\" means the files and folders inside. Read the path in the message. Answer `Y` only if it is the right folder.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS**: `rmdir old-drafts` removes an empty folder. `rm -r -i old-drafts` removes a full folder and asks about each item. Never add `-f` (force), which turns off all questions.\n\n## 🛡️ The Three Golden Rules\n\n1. **Check where you are.** Run `cd` (CMD) or `pwd` (PowerShell) first.\n2. **List before you delete.** Run `dir` and read the names. Planning to delete all `.log` files with `del *.log`? Run `dir *.log` first. The `*` means \"any name\", so this shows every file the delete would remove.\n3. **Delete one named item at a time,** and let the terminal ask you to confirm.\n\nLater, in the Git book, you will learn to save versions of your work. Git can bring back a file that was saved in a version. Until then, the only safety is care.\n\n## 🤝 Digital Trust (*Amānah*)\n\nIn Islam, what is placed in our care is an **Amānah**, a trust. A shared school computer is a trust. So are other people's files.\n\n- Never delete another student's or teacher's files.\n- Never try deleting commands on shared or family computers. Use only your practice folder.\n- Never pass on a \"trick\" command to others without understanding it. Tricking someone into harming their computer breaks their trust.\n- Keep backups of work that matters, such as homework and projects.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Deleting in the wrong folder.** Bilal wants to tidy his practice folder. But his terminal is still in `C:\\Users\\Bilal\\Documents`. He types `del report.txt` without looking. His real school report is gone, with no Recycle Bin copy.\n\nThe fixed habit:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Bilal\\Documents>cd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>cd\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>dir /b report.txt\nreport.txt\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>del /p report.txt\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\report.txt, Delete (Y/N)? Y\n```\n\nMove, check, list, then delete with a question.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nInside `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice`:\n\n1. Make a file called `junk.txt`.\n2. List the folder to see it.\n3. Delete it with a confirmation question (`/p` or `-Confirm`).\n4. List again to check it is gone.\n\n*Hint*: Use `type nul > junk.txt` (CMD) or `New-Item junk.txt` (PowerShell) from Chapter 7.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- `del name` deletes one file. It skips the Recycle Bin.\n- `del /p` (CMD) and `del -Confirm` (PowerShell) ask before deleting.\n- `rmdir` removes only empty folders. That refusal protects you.\n- `rmdir /s` removes a full folder after asking. We never add `/q`.\n- Always: check where you are, list first, delete one named item, and only in your practice folder.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\n1. Which command deletes a file in CMD and PowerShell? Which one in Git Bash?\n2. What happens if you use `rmdir` on a folder that still has files in it?\n3. Where does a file go after `del notes.txt`?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `del` in CMD and PowerShell. `rm` in Git Bash.\n2. CMD refuses with `The directory is not empty.` PowerShell stops and asks for confirmation.\n3. Nowhere you can reach. It does **not** go to the Recycle Bin. It is gone.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nYour practice folder contains `draft1.txt`, `draft2.txt`, `final.txt` and an empty folder `tmp`.\n\n1. Write the command that shows which files start with `draft`.\n2. Write the commands that delete only the two drafts, asking each time.\n3. Remove the empty `tmp` folder.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `dir draft*` (both terminals).\n2. CMD: `del /p draft1.txt` then `del /p draft2.txt`. PowerShell: `del draft1.txt -Confirm` then `del draft2.txt -Confirm`.\n3. `rmdir tmp`.\n\n`final.txt` is never touched.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA message in a chat group says: *\"Open CMD in `C:\\` and run `del` with the options `/s /q` on every file name. Your computer will run twice as fast!\"*\n\n1. What would that really do?\n2. Why is the `/q` part extra dangerous?\n3. Why does our code of digital ethics forbid sharing such \"tricks\"?\n4. What should you do when you see a message like this?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. It would try to delete every file it is allowed to, in every folder of the drive: schoolwork, programs, settings. Nothing goes to the Recycle Bin. The computer would not be faster. It could stop working properly.\n2. `/q` removes the \"Are you sure?\" question, so there is no chance to stop.\n3. It is deception that harms people and their property. It breaks the trust (*Amānah*) others place in us, and it is a lie (against *Ṣidq*, truthfulness).\n4. Do not run it. Do not forward it. Tell a teacher or a trusted adult, and warn the person who shared it kindly.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-04-the-web-developer-workflow/ch10-launching-vs-code-and-tools.md": "# Chapter 10: Launching VS Code & Verifying Developer Tools\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nBefore a science lesson, the lab assistant checks the shelf. Is the microscope there? Are the test tubes clean? Is there enough of each liquid? She checks first, so the lesson does not stop halfway. Developers do the same. Before a project starts, they check their tools from the terminal. Then they open their workspace with one short command.\n\n> **Key Idea**: The terminal is the launch pad for your developer tools. One command opens VS Code in your project folder. A few more check that Git, PHP, Node.js and Python are ready.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Open the current folder in VS Code with `code .`.\n- Check which version of a tool is installed.\n- Understand the \"not recognized\" error and what it really means.\n- Find where a program lives with `where.exe` and `Get-Command`.\n- Know what to do (and not do) when a tool is missing.\n\n## 🚀 Opening VS Code: `code .`\n\n**VS Code** (Visual Studio Code) is a free code editor. Many beginners open it, click *File*, then *Open Folder*, and hunt through menus. From the terminal it takes two seconds.\n\n1. Go to your project folder.\n2. Type `code .` and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>cd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\portfolio\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\portfolio>code .\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\portfolio>\n```\n\n**PowerShell**:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\Users\\Amina> cd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\portfolio\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\portfolio> code .\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\portfolio>\n```\n\nThe terminal prints nothing, and the prompt comes back. A moment later, VS Code opens with `portfolio` in its side bar.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Remember Chapter 5: `.` means *\"this folder\"*. So `code .` means *\"VS Code, please open the folder I am standing in.\"* You can also open one file: `code index.html`.\n\n## 🧰 Checking Tool Versions\n\nEvery developer tool can tell you its **version** number. A version shows which edition of the tool you have. Most tools use the option `--version`, and some also accept `-v`.\n\n| Tool | What it is for | Command |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Git | Saving versions of your work | `git --version` |\n| Node.js | Running JavaScript outside the browser | `node -v` |\n| npm | Installing JavaScript libraries | `npm -v` |\n| PHP | Running PHP code | `php -v` |\n| Python | Running Python code | `python --version` |\n| VS Code | Code editor | `code --version` |\n\nThese commands look the same in CMD and PowerShell. Here is what one lab computer printed:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>git --version\ngit version 2.54.0.windows.1\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>node -v\nv24.17.0\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>python --version\nPython 3.12.10\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>php -v\nPHP 8.3.31 (cli) (built: May  5 2026 17:23:34) (ZTS Visual C++ 2019 x64)\nCopyright (c) The PHP Group\nZend Engine v4.3.31, Copyright (c) Zend Technologies\n```\n\nYour numbers will probably be different. That is normal. Tools get new versions often. What matters is that each command printed a version, so the tool is ready.\n\n## ❓ \"Not Recognized\": What It Means\n\nSometimes you get an error instead of a version.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>php -v\n'php' is not recognized as an internal or external command,\noperable program or batch file.\n```\n\n**PowerShell** (first lines of the red message):\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice> php -v\nphp : The term 'php' is not recognized as the name of a cmdlet, function, script file, or operable\nprogram. Check the spelling of the name, or if a path was included, verify that the path is correct and try again.\n```\n\nDo not worry. Nothing is broken. There are only three common reasons.\n\n1. **A spelling mistake.** Check every letter: `phpp`, `gti` and `pyhton` are easy typos.\n2. **The tool is not installed** on this computer.\n3. **The tool is installed, but Windows does not know where to look.** Windows keeps a list of folders to search for programs. This list is called the **PATH**. If the tool's folder is not in the PATH, typing its name does not find it.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Do not change the PATH or install software yourself on a school computer.** These are system settings. Changing them often needs Administrator rights, and we never run as Administrator. Write down the tool name and the error, and ask your teacher.\n\n## 📍 Where Does a Program Live?\n\nYou can ask Windows where it finds a program.\n\n**CMD** uses `where`:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>where git\nC:\\Program Files\\Git\\cmd\\git.exe\n```\n\nIf the program cannot be found:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>where php\nINFO: Could not find files for the given pattern(s).\n```\n\n**PowerShell**: careful! In PowerShell, `where` means something else (it filters lists), so `where git` prints nothing. Type `where.exe` instead, or use `Get-Command`:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice> where.exe git\nC:\\Program Files\\Git\\cmd\\git.exe\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice> Get-Command git\n\nCommandType     Name          Version    Source\n-----------     ----          -------    ------\nApplication     git.exe       2.54.0.1   C:\\Program Files\\Git\\cmd\\git.exe\n```\n\nThe `Source` column shows where the program lives.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS** use `which`:\n> ```text\n> $ which git\n> /mingw64/bin/git\n> ```\n> On a Mac, it usually prints `/usr/bin/git`.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Forgetting the dot.** Amina types `code` without the dot:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\portfolio>code\n```\n\nVS Code opens, but it shows whatever it had open last time, not her project. The dot is the important part. Fixed:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\portfolio>code .\n```\n\n**Using one hyphen for a long option.** `git -version` fails, because long word options need **two** hyphens:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>git -version\nunknown option: -version\nusage: git [-v | --version] [-h | --help] [-C <path>] [-c <name>=<value>]\n...\n```\n\nFixed: `git --version`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nMake a small \"tool check\" record for your computer.\n\n1. Run `git --version`, `node -v`, `php -v` and `python --version`.\n2. For each one, write **ready** and the version, or **missing**.\n3. For any **ready** tool, find where it lives with `where` (CMD) or `where.exe` (PowerShell).\n4. Open your practice folder in VS Code with `code .`.\n\n*Hint*: A missing tool is not a failure. It is useful information for your teacher.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- `code .` opens the current folder in VS Code. `.` means \"this folder\".\n- `--version` (or `-v`) shows a tool's version. Different numbers on different computers are normal.\n- \"Not recognized\" means a typo, a missing tool, or a tool that is not in the PATH.\n- `where git` (CMD), `where.exe git` or `Get-Command git` (PowerShell), `which git` (Git Bash) show where a program lives.\n- Never change the PATH or install software yourself on a school computer. Ask your teacher.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\n1. Which command opens VS Code with the current folder loaded?\n2. Which option do most tools accept to show their version?\n3. Which CMD command shows where a program is stored? Why must PowerShell users type it differently?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `code .`\n2. `--version` (many also accept `-v`).\n3. `where`. In PowerShell, `where` is a different command that filters lists, so you type `where.exe` (or use `Get-Command`).\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nHamza runs these commands and sees:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>git --version\ngit version 2.54.0.windows.1\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>pyhton --version\n'pyhton' is not recognized as an internal or external command,\noperable program or batch file.\n```\n\n1. Is Git ready?\n2. Is Python missing? What should Hamza try first?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. Yes. Git printed a version, so it is installed and found.\n2. We cannot tell yet. He typed `pyhton`, which is a spelling mistake. He should first try the correct spelling, `python --version`. Only if that also fails is Python missing or not in the PATH.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA student has XAMPP. In CMD, `php -v` says *\"not recognized\"*. But this works:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Sara>cd C:\\xampp\\php\n\nC:\\xampp\\php>php -v\nPHP 8.2.12 (cli) ...more details on the same line...\n```\n\n1. Why does `php -v` work inside `C:\\xampp\\php` but nowhere else?\n2. Who should fix this on a school computer, and why?\n3. Until it is fixed, how can she still run PHP from her project folder?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. CMD always looks in the **current folder** first. `php.exe` is inside `C:\\xampp\\php`, so it is found there. From other folders, CMD searches only the folders in the PATH, and `C:\\xampp\\php` is not on that list.\n2. The teacher or the school's computer technician. Changing the PATH is a system setting. Students should not change system settings or use Administrator rights.\n3. She can type the program's absolute path (Chapter 5): `C:\\xampp\\php\\php.exe -v`. That works from any folder.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-04-the-web-developer-workflow/ch11-local-servers-and-ctrl-c.md": "# Chapter 11: Local Development Servers & The Emergency Brake (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd>)\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nDuring the school book fair, Khadija runs the information desk. While the desk is open, she is busy answering visitors. She cannot leave to do other jobs. At the end of the day, the teacher rings a bell. Khadija closes the desk, tidies up, and is free again. A local web server is like that desk. While it runs, it is busy answering your browser. <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd> is the bell that closes it.\n\n> **Key Idea**: Some web pages, such as PHP pages, need a **web server** to work. The terminal can start a small server in seconds. <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd> stops it and gives you your prompt back.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what a local web server is, and what `localhost` means.\n- Start PHP's built-in server and Python's simple server.\n- Read the lines a server prints while it works.\n- Stop a server, or any stuck command, with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd>.\n\n## 🖥️ What Is a Local Server?\n\nA **web server** is a program that sends web pages to browsers. Big websites use servers on computers far away. A **local server** runs on **your own** computer, just for you to test your work.\n\nIts address is `localhost`. That name always means *\"this computer\"*. After it comes a **port** number, like `:8000`. A port is like a numbered door. It lets several programs on one computer receive messages without mixing them up.\n\nPlain HTML pages open fine with a double-click. But a **PHP** page needs a server to run its PHP code first. Without a server, the browser only shows the raw code, or offers to download the file.\n\n## 🐘 Starting PHP's Built-In Server\n\nPHP comes with a small server for testing. First go to your website folder. Make sure it has an `index.html` (or `index.php`). Then start the server with `php -S localhost:8000`. The `-S` must be a **capital** S. It means **S**erver.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>cd my-site\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-site>php -S localhost:8000\n[Tue Oct 13 08:05:19 2026] PHP 8.3.31 Development Server (http://localhost:8000) started\n```\n\n**PowerShell** uses the same command:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-site> php -S localhost:8000\n[Tue Oct 13 08:05:19 2026] PHP 8.3.31 Development Server (http://localhost:8000) started\n```\n\nYour date and PHP version will be different. Notice: **the prompt does not come back.** The server is now running in this window.\n\nOpen your browser and go to `http://localhost:8000`. Your page appears! Look back at the terminal. The server has written a few new lines:\n\n```text\n[Tue Oct 13 08:05:22 2026] [::1]:54938 Accepted\n[Tue Oct 13 08:05:22 2026] [::1]:54938 [200]: GET /\n[Tue Oct 13 08:05:22 2026] [::1]:54938 Closing\n```\n\n- **Accepted**: a browser connected. `::1` is another way to write \"this computer\".\n- **`[200]: GET /`**: the browser asked (`GET`) for the main page (`/`). The code `200` means *\"OK, here it is\"*.\n- **Closing**: the answer was sent.\n\nEach time you refresh the page, more lines appear. This list is called a **log**. It is a diary of every visit.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS**: the command is the same, `php -S localhost:8000`, if PHP is installed.\n\n## 🐍 Python's Simple Server\n\nIf Python is installed, it can also serve plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript files (but not PHP). The command is the same in CMD and PowerShell:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-site>python -m http.server 8000\nServing HTTP on :: port 8000 (http://[::]:8000/) ...\n```\n\n`-m http.server` means *\"run Python's built-in **http.server** tool\"*. Open `http://localhost:8000` in the browser. Each visit adds a log line like this:\n\n```text\n::1 - - [13/Oct/2026 08:05:22] \"GET / HTTP/1.1\" 200 -\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS**: the same command works. On a Mac, type `python3` instead of `python`.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Windows may show a firewall question the first time. You do not need to allow anything to visit `localhost`. If you are not sure, ask your teacher before clicking.\n\n## 🛑 The Emergency Brake: <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd>\n\nWhile a server runs, you **cannot type new commands** in that window. The terminal is busy listening for browsers.\n\nTo stop it, click inside the terminal window and press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd>. The server stops and the prompt returns.\n\n**PHP server in CMD**:\n\n```text\n[Tue Oct 13 08:05:22 2026] [::1]:54938 Closing\n^C\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-site>\n```\n\n**Python server**:\n\n```text\n::1 - - [13/Oct/2026 08:05:22] \"GET / HTTP/1.1\" 200 -\n^C\nKeyboard interrupt received, exiting.\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-site>\n```\n\n`^C` is how the terminal shows that you pressed <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd>. In PowerShell you may not see `^C`, but the prompt `PS ...>` comes back the same way. Now refresh the browser. The page no longer loads, because the server is closed.\n\n### Other times to use <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd>\n\n- A program keeps printing lines and will not stop.\n- A command is waiting for something, and you want to cancel it.\n- A command is taking far too long.\n\n<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd> works in CMD, PowerShell, Git Bash and macOS. It is the most important \"stop\" key in the terminal.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**A small `s` instead of a capital `S`.**\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\my-site>php -s localhost:8000\nCould not open input file: localhost:8000\n```\n\nSmall `-s` is a different option. PHP thinks `localhost:8000` is a file name. Fixed: `php -S localhost:8000`.\n\n**Starting the server in the wrong folder.** Bilal starts the server in an empty folder. His browser shows **Not Found**, and the log says:\n\n```text\n[Tue Oct 13 08:18:56 2026] [::1]:57871 [404]: GET / - No such file or directory\n```\n\n`404` means *\"I cannot find that page\"*. The server serves the folder you **started it in**. Fix: press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd>, `cd` into the website folder, then press <kbd>↑</kbd> to bring back the start command and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\n1. In `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice`, make a folder `my-site` and go into it.\n2. Run `echo ^<h1^>Hello from Localhost^</h1^> > index.html` in CMD, or `echo '<h1>Hello from Localhost</h1>' > index.html` in PowerShell.\n3. Start a server with `php -S localhost:8000` (or `python -m http.server 8000`).\n4. Visit `http://localhost:8000`. Then read the new log lines.\n5. Stop the server with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd>. Refresh the browser to prove it stopped.\n\n*Hint*: In CMD, `<` and `>` are special signs. The `^` in front of each one tells CMD to treat it as normal text.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- A **web server** sends pages to browsers. A **local server** runs on your own computer at `localhost`.\n- `php -S localhost:8000` starts PHP's server (capital `S`). `python -m http.server 8000` starts Python's.\n- While a server runs, the prompt does not come back. The window shows a log of every visit.\n- `200` means OK. `404` means not found.\n- <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd> stops the server, or any stuck command, and returns the prompt.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\n1. Which command starts PHP's built-in server on port 8000?\n2. Which keys stop a running server?\n3. What happens to the prompt while a server is running?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `php -S localhost:8000`\n2. <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd>.\n3. It does not come back. The window is busy with the server until you stop it.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nRead this server log:\n\n```text\n[Tue Oct 13 09:10:02 2026] [::1]:60110 [200]: GET /\n[Tue Oct 13 09:10:02 2026] [::1]:60111 [200]: GET /style.css\n[Tue Oct 13 09:10:03 2026] [::1]:60112 [404]: GET /logo.png - No such file or directory\n```\n\n1. Which files did the browser receive?\n2. Which file was missing?\n3. Suggest two reasons why it was missing.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. The main page (`/`) and `style.css`.\n2. `logo.png` (code `404`).\n3. For example: the picture is in a different folder, such as `images\\logo.png`; the name is spelled differently (`Logo.png` or `logo.jpg`); or the file was never copied into the site folder. Use `dir` to check.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nMariam has a page `index.php` that shows today's date using PHP.\n\n1. She double-clicks the file in File Explorer. What does she see, and why?\n2. Write the steps (with commands) to view it properly, in CMD or PowerShell.\n3. When she has finished, how does she shut everything down correctly?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. The browser shows the raw PHP code, or offers to download the file. A browser cannot run PHP. Only a server with PHP can run it and send back the finished HTML.\n2. Steps:\n\n```text\ncd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\mariam-site\nphp -S localhost:8000\n```\n\nThen open `http://localhost:8000` in the browser. (Python's server would not work here, because it does not run PHP.)\n\n3. Click in the terminal window and press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd>. The prompt comes back. Refresh the browser to check the server has stopped.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-04-the-web-developer-workflow/ch12-reading-and-piping-basics.md": "# Chapter 12: Reading File Contents & Output Redirection\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nThe school library keeps a daily notebook. Each day the librarian writes one new line at the bottom: when it opened, how many books came back, any problems. She never tears out old pages. When the head teacher asks, \"Were there any problems this week?\", she runs her finger down the pages and reads only the lines about problems. In this chapter, you will do all of this from the terminal: read a file, add lines to the end, and find just the lines you need.\n\n> **Key Idea**: You can read a short file without opening an editor. You can send a command's output **into** a file with `>` and `>>`. And you can pass one command's output **to** another command with the **pipe** `|`.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Print a file on the screen, all at once or page by page.\n- Show only the first or last lines of a file.\n- Tell the difference between `>` (replace) and `>>` (add to the end).\n- Search for words inside a file.\n- Join two commands with a pipe `|`.\n\n## 📖 Printing a File on the Screen\n\n| Task | CMD | PowerShell |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Print the whole file | `type log.txt` | `type log.txt` or `Get-Content log.txt` |\n| One screen at a time | `more log.txt` | `more log.txt` |\n| First 2 lines | (no simple way) | `Get-Content log.txt -Head 2` |\n| Last line | (no simple way) | `Get-Content log.txt -Tail 1` |\n\nHere is the library's `log.txt`, printed in **CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library>type log.txt\nLibrary opened\nError: printer offline\nBooks returned: 12\nerror: scanner jammed\n```\n\nIn **PowerShell**, `type` is a short name for `Get-Content`. The result is the same.\n\nFor long files, `more` shows one screenful, then waits. Press <kbd>Space</kbd> for the next page, or <kbd>Q</kbd> to stop.\n\nThe first and last lines in **PowerShell**:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library> Get-Content log.txt -Head 2\nLibrary opened\nError: printer offline\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library> Get-Content log.txt -Tail 1\nerror: scanner jammed\n```\n\n`-Tail` is very useful for logs, where the newest lines are at the bottom.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS**: `cat log.txt` prints a file. `less log.txt` shows it page by page (<kbd>Q</kbd> quits). `head -n 2 log.txt` and `tail -n 1 log.txt` show the first and last lines.\n\n## ➡️ Redirection: `>` and `>>`\n\nMost commands print their result on the screen. **Redirection** sends that result into a file instead.\n\n- **`>`** creates the file, or **replaces everything** already in it.\n- **`>>`** **adds** to the end of the file and keeps what was there.\n\nYou met `>` in Chapter 7. Now let us build a file one line at a time.\n\n**CMD**:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library>echo Reading Club Project> README.md\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library>echo Made by Year 7 volunteers>> README.md\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library>echo License: MIT>> README.md\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library>type README.md\nReading Club Project\nMade by Year 7 volunteers\nLicense: MIT\n```\n\nNotice there is no space before `>` and `>>`. In CMD, a space there would be saved at the end of the line.\n\n**PowerShell** (put the text in quotes):\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library> echo 'Reading Club Project' > README.md\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library> echo 'Made by Year 7 volunteers' >> README.md\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library> echo 'License: MIT' >> README.md\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library> type README.md\nReading Club Project\nMade by Year 7 volunteers\nLicense: MIT\n```\n\nThe first command used `>` to start a fresh file. The next two used `>>` to add lines. If you used `>` for all three, only `License: MIT` would be left.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS** use `>` and `>>` the same way: `echo \"License: MIT\" >> README.md`.\n\n## 🔍 Finding Words Inside a File\n\n| Task | CMD | PowerShell |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Find a word | `findstr Error log.txt` | `Select-String Error log.txt` |\n| Ignore capital letters | `findstr /i error log.txt` | `Select-String error log.txt` |\n| Show line numbers | `findstr /n Error log.txt` | (always shown) |\n\n**CMD** is exact about capital letters unless you add `/i` (**i**gnore case):\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library>findstr Error log.txt\nError: printer offline\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library>findstr /i /n error log.txt\n2:Error: printer offline\n4:error: scanner jammed\n```\n\n**PowerShell**'s `Select-String` ignores capital letters by itself. It shows the file name and line number too:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library> Select-String error log.txt\n\nlog.txt:2:Error: printer offline\nlog.txt:4:error: scanner jammed\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS**: `grep Error log.txt` finds a word. `grep -i -n error log.txt` ignores capitals and shows line numbers.\n\n## 🔗 The Pipe: `|`\n\nThe **pipe** sign `|` connects two commands. The output of the first command becomes the input of the second. It is like a water pipe carrying water from one tank to the next. On most keyboards, `|` is <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>\\</kbd>.\n\n**CMD**: print the log, but keep only the lines with \"Books\":\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library>type log.txt | findstr Books\nBooks returned: 12\n```\n\n**PowerShell**:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library> Get-Content log.txt | Select-String Books\n\nBooks returned: 12\n```\n\nPipes also work with listings. This shows only the names that contain `html`:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library>dir /b | findstr html\nindex.html\n```\n\nIn PowerShell: `dir -Name | Select-String html`. And for a very long listing, `dir | more` shows it one page at a time.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Using `>` when you meant `>>`.** Hassan wants to add a line to the library's book list:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library>echo The Night Sky Atlas> books.txt\n```\n\nThe list had 40 titles. Now it has one. There is no warning and no Recycle Bin. Fixed:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library>echo The Night Sky Atlas>> books.txt\n```\n\nBefore using `>`, ask: *\"Is this file new, or do I want to replace it?\"*\n\n**`#` in PowerShell without quotes.** In PowerShell, `#` starts a **comment**. Everything after it is ignored. Fatima wants her README to start with a title line, `# Reading Club Project`. She types:\n\n```text\nPS C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library> echo # Reading Club Project > README.md\n\ncmdlet Write-Output at command pipeline position 1\nSupply values for the following parameters:\nInputObject[0]:\n```\n\nPowerShell saw only `echo` and is now waiting for text. Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd> to cancel, then use quotes: `echo '# Reading Club Project' > README.md`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nIn `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice`, make a folder `library` and go into it. Then:\n\n1. Create `log.txt` with the line `System started` (use `>`).\n2. Add `Database connected` and then `Error: backup failed` (use `>>`).\n3. Print the whole file.\n4. Show only the line with `Error`.\n\n*Hint*: In PowerShell, use quotes around each text. In CMD, leave no space before `>`.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- `type` prints a file in CMD and PowerShell. `more` shows it page by page.\n- `Get-Content file -Head N` and `-Tail N` show the first or last lines in PowerShell.\n- `>` creates or **replaces** a file. `>>` **adds** to the end.\n- `findstr` (CMD) and `Select-String` (PowerShell) find words inside files.\n- The pipe `|` sends one command's output into the next command.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\n1. Which command prints a file in CMD? Which one in Git Bash?\n2. What is the difference between `>` and `>>`?\n3. Which command searches inside files in CMD? And in PowerShell?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `type` in CMD (also in PowerShell). `cat` in Git Bash.\n2. `>` replaces everything in the file. `>>` adds new lines at the end and keeps the old ones.\n3. `findstr` in CMD. `Select-String` in PowerShell.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nA file `visits.txt` holds:\n\n```text\nMonday: 31 visitors\nTuesday: 27 visitors\nWednesday: closed for cleaning\nThursday: 35 visitors\n```\n\n1. Write a CMD command that shows only the Wednesday line.\n2. Write a PowerShell command that shows only the last two lines.\n3. Write a CMD command that adds `Friday: 40 visitors` without losing anything.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `findstr Wednesday visits.txt` (or `type visits.txt | findstr Wednesday`). Output: `Wednesday: closed for cleaning`.\n2. `Get-Content visits.txt -Tail 2`. Output: the Wednesday and Thursday lines.\n3. `echo Friday: 40 visitors>> visits.txt`\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA developer meant to type `echo done >> important-data.sql` but typed `echo done > important-data.sql`.\n\n1. What happened to `important-data.sql`?\n2. How could a pipe and `findstr` have helped them check the file **before** changing it?\n3. Write two habits that protect files from this mistake. How do they show *Iḥsān* (careful, excellent work)?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. All the data was replaced. The file now holds only the word `done`. It did not go to the Recycle Bin.\n2. For example, `type important-data.sql | more` to look at it first, or `findstr done important-data.sql` to see whether the line is already there. Looking first would remind them the file is valuable.\n3. Habits:\n   - Read the line before pressing Enter, and check `>` versus `>>`.\n   - Make a backup copy first: `copy important-data.sql important-data.backup.sql`.\n\n*Iḥsān* means doing work as if it matters, because it does. Slowing down for a second protects other people's data, which is a trust (*Amānah*).\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-05-capstone-challenges/ch13-scaffolding-capstone.md": "# Chapter 13: Capstone Challenge 1: The 60-Second Full-Stack Scaffolder\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nBefore a community food drive, volunteers set up the hall. One group puts out tables. Another labels the boxes: rice, lentils, flour, tinned food. A third puts up signs. Because everyone follows a plan, the hall is ready in minutes. Starting a website is the same. Before writing any content, a developer sets up the folders and empty files, called the **scaffold**. Like the scaffolding on a building, it is the frame that everything else hangs on.\n\n> **Key Idea**: Put all your skills together. Using only the terminal, build the full structure of a website, write its first page, check it, and open it in VS Code. No mouse!\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Plan a project's folders and files before typing.\n- Combine `cd`, `mkdir`, empty files, `echo`, `>` and `>>` in one task.\n- Check a finished structure with `tree /f`, `dir /s /b` or `ls -Recurse -Name`.\n- Open the finished project in VS Code and view it on a local server.\n\n## 🎯 The Mission\n\nA community charity called **Kindness Pantry** shares food with families in need. They need a simple website. Your job is to build its scaffold inside `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice`:\n\n```text\nkindness-pantry\n├── index.html\n├── contact.html\n├── README.md\n├── css\n│   ├── style.css\n│   └── reset.css\n├── js\n│   └── app.js\n└── assets\n    └── images\n```\n\n**Rule**: no File Explorer and no mouse until the very end.\n\n## 🛠️ The Plan\n\nGood builders plan first. Here are the steps, with the chapter where you learned each skill.\n\n1. Go to the practice folder (Chapter 4).\n2. Make the project folder and go into it (Chapter 7).\n3. Make all the subfolders (Chapter 7).\n4. Make the empty files (Chapter 7).\n5. Write a starter `README.md` and `index.html` (Chapter 12).\n6. Check the structure (Chapters 3 and 7).\n7. Open it in VS Code and on a local server (Chapters 10 and 11).\n\n## 💻 Solution in CMD\n\nLines starting with `::` are **comments**. They are notes for people. CMD ignores them. You do not need to type them.\n\n```text\n:: 1. Go to the practice folder\ncd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\n\n:: 2. Make the project folder and go in\nmkdir kindness-pantry\ncd kindness-pantry\n\n:: 3. Make all subfolders (CMD makes assets too)\nmkdir css js assets\\images\n\n:: 4. Make the empty files\ntype nul > contact.html\ntype nul > css\\style.css\ntype nul > css\\reset.css\ntype nul > js\\app.js\n\n:: 5. Write README.md and index.html\necho # Kindness Pantry> README.md\necho ^<!DOCTYPE html^> > index.html\necho ^<html lang=\"en\"^> >> index.html\necho ^<h1^>Welcome to Kindness Pantry^</h1^> >> index.html\necho ^</html^> >> index.html\n```\n\nIn CMD, `<` and `>` have special jobs. The `^` sign before each one says *\"this is just a normal letter\"*. The last `>` or `>>` on each line, without `^`, is the real redirect.\n\nNow print the page to check it:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\kindness-pantry>type index.html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<h1>Welcome to Kindness Pantry</h1>\n</html>\n```\n\n## 💻 Solution in PowerShell\n\nThe steps are in the same order as the CMD solution. Watch for the commas when making folders and files, and the quotes around the text.\n\n```text\ncd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\nmkdir kindness-pantry\ncd kindness-pantry\nmkdir css, js, assets\\images\nNew-Item contact.html, css\\style.css, css\\reset.css, js\\app.js\necho '# Kindness Pantry' > README.md\necho '<!DOCTYPE html>' > index.html\necho '<html lang=\"en\">' >> index.html\necho '<h1>Welcome to Kindness Pantry</h1>' >> index.html\necho '</html>' >> index.html\n```\n\n`New-Item` can make several files at once when you separate the names with commas. The single quotes `'...'` keep the `<`, `>` and `#` signs as plain text, so no `^` is needed.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS**:\n> ```text\n> cd /c/JuniorCoders/practice\n> mkdir kindness-pantry && cd kindness-pantry\n> mkdir -p css js assets/images\n> touch contact.html css/style.css css/reset.css js/app.js\n> echo \"# Kindness Pantry\" > README.md\n> echo '<h1>Welcome to Kindness Pantry</h1>' > index.html\n> ```\n> `&&` means *\"if the first command worked, run the second\"*. On a Mac, start with `cd ~` and work in a practice folder in your home folder.\n\n## ✅ Checking Your Work\n\nThe `tree /f` command draws the folder as a tree, with **f**iles included. It works in both CMD and PowerShell:\n\n```text\n...\\kindness-pantry>tree /f\nFolder PATH listing\nVolume serial number is 1A2B-3C4D\nC:.\n│   contact.html\n│   index.html\n│   README.md\n│\n├───assets\n│   └───images\n├───css\n│       reset.css\n│       style.css\n│\n└───js\n        app.js\n```\n\n`C:.` means *\"this folder\"*. Compare the tree with the plan at the top. Everything is there.\n\nYou can also use `dir /s /b` (CMD) or `ls -Recurse -Name` (PowerShell) from Chapter 7.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS**: `ls -R` lists every folder and its contents.\n\n## 🚀 Final Step: Open and View\n\n```text\ncode .\nphp -S localhost:8000\n```\n\n`code .` opens the project in VS Code. Check the side bar matches your tree. Then start the server and visit `http://localhost:8000` to see your heading. Stop the server with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd> when you are done.\n\n## ⏱️ Mastery Levels\n\nTime yourself. Focus on **correct** first, then on **fast**.\n\n- 🥉 **Apprentice**: the whole scaffold is correct, with no errors.\n- 🥈 **Proficient**: correct in under 2 minutes.\n- 🥇 **Master**: correct in under 60 seconds, using <kbd>Tab</kbd> and <kbd>↑</kbd> to save typing.\n\nType every command yourself. Copying and pasting builds nothing in your memory, and honest practice is part of academic integrity.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Forgetting to `cd` into the project folder.** Hamza makes `kindness-pantry`, but forgets `cd kindness-pantry`. All his `css`, `js` and `index.html` land in `C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice` instead.\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>mkdir kindness-pantry\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice>mkdir css js assets\\images\n```\n\nLook at the prompt! It still says `practice`. The fix: check the prompt before each step. To tidy up, move the items into the project folder with `move` (Chapter 8), for example `move css kindness-pantry\\`.\n\n**Using `>` for every line of `index.html`.** Only the last line survives. The first line uses `>`; every line after it uses `>>` (Chapter 12).\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nBuild a second scaffold for a **community garden** website, called `green-corner`:\n\n```text\ngreen-corner\n├── index.html\n├── volunteers.html\n├── css\n│   └── style.css\n└── images\n```\n\nWrite `<h1>Green Corner Community Garden</h1>` into `index.html`. Check it with `tree /f`.\n\n*Hint*: Follow the same seven-step plan. In CMD, remember `^` before `<` and `>` inside the text.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- A **scaffold** is a project's frame: its folders and starting files.\n- Plan the tree first, then build it step by step.\n- CMD needs `^` before `<` and `>` inside `echo` text. PowerShell needs quotes instead.\n- `>` starts a file, `>>` adds each next line.\n- `tree /f` draws your finished structure so you can compare it with the plan.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\n1. What is a project scaffold?\n2. Which command draws a folder as a tree, including files?\n3. In CMD, why do we write `^<h1^>` instead of `<h1>` after `echo`?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. The starting structure of a project: its folders and empty files, ready to be filled.\n2. `tree /f` (in CMD and PowerShell).\n3. `<` and `>` are redirect signs in CMD. The `^` tells CMD to treat them as normal letters, so they are written into the file.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWrite the commands (CMD **or** PowerShell) to build this inside your practice folder:\n\n```text\nstudy-planner\n├── index.html\n├── css\n│   └── planner.css\n└── js\n    └── timer.js\n```\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nCMD:\n\n```text\ncd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\nmkdir study-planner\ncd study-planner\nmkdir css js\ntype nul > index.html\ntype nul > css\\planner.css\ntype nul > js\\timer.js\ntree /f\n```\n\nPowerShell:\n\n```text\ncd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\nmkdir study-planner\ncd study-planner\nmkdir css, js\nNew-Item index.html, css\\planner.css, js\\timer.js\ntree /f\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nYour school's science club wants a website for its annual fair. Design and build it.\n\n1. Draw the tree first. It must include a home page, a page for projects, a page for the fair timetable, a `css` folder with one style file, and an `images` folder with a `projects` folder inside.\n2. Build it with the terminal only.\n3. Write a heading into the home page and a title line into `README.md`.\n4. Check it with `tree /f`, open it with `code .`, and view it on a local server.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\nPlan:\n\n```text\nscience-fair\n├── index.html\n├── projects.html\n├── timetable.html\n├── README.md\n├── css\n│   └── style.css\n└── images\n    └── projects\n```\n\nPowerShell:\n\n```text\ncd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\nmkdir science-fair\ncd science-fair\nmkdir css, images\\projects\nNew-Item projects.html, timetable.html, css\\style.css\necho '# Science Fair Website' > README.md\necho '<h1>Annual Science Fair</h1>' > index.html\ntree /f\ncode .\nphp -S localhost:8000\n```\n\nIn CMD, use `mkdir css images\\projects`, `type nul >` for each empty file, and `echo ^<h1^>Annual Science Fair^</h1^> > index.html`. Stop the server with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd>.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-05-capstone-challenges/ch14-detective-troubleshooting.md": "# Chapter 14: Capstone Challenge 2: The Broken Asset Path Detective\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nWhen a patient visits the doctor, the doctor does not guess. She asks questions, looks carefully, and checks the clues. Only then does she decide what to do. After the treatment, she checks again that the patient is better. Fixing a broken website works the same way. In this final chapter you are the detective. You will use clues from the terminal to find what is wrong, fix it, and prove it is fixed.\n\n> **Key Idea**: Troubleshooting is a calm routine: **read** the error, **check** where you are, **list** what is there, **compare** it with what is expected, **fix** one thing at a time, and **check** again.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Follow a six-step detective routine for any terminal problem.\n- Understand the most common error messages in CMD and PowerShell.\n- Find and fix broken file paths in a real website folder.\n- Remove an unwanted folder safely, after checking it first.\n- Review everything this book has taught you.\n\n## 🕵️ The Detective Routine\n\n| Step | Ask yourself | Command |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 1. Read | What does the error say, exactly? | (read slowly) |\n| 2. Locate | Where am I standing? | `cd` (CMD) / `pwd` (PowerShell) |\n| 3. List | What is really here? | `dir`, `tree /f` |\n| 4. Compare | What did the page or command expect? | `type file` |\n| 5. Fix | What is the smallest change that fixes it? | `mkdir`, `move`, `ren` |\n| 6. Check | Did it work? | `dir`, `tree /f`, browser |\n\n## 📚 Error Message Guide\n\nThese messages are clues, not disasters. Each one tells you what to check.\n\n| Message | Terminal | Usual meaning |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `'xyz' is not recognized as an internal or external command` | CMD | Typo, or tool not installed / not in PATH |\n| `The term 'xyz' is not recognized as the name of a cmdlet` | PowerShell | Same as above |\n| `The system cannot find the path specified.` | CMD | A folder in the path does not exist here |\n| `Cannot find path '...' because it does not exist.` | PowerShell | Same as above |\n| `File Not Found` / `Could Not Find ...` | CMD | No file with that name here |\n| `A subdirectory or file css already exists.` | CMD | Nothing to do: it is already there |\n| `The directory is not empty.` | CMD | `rmdir` protected a folder that still has files |\n| `[404]: GET /...` in a server log | PHP server | The browser asked for a file that is not there |\n\n## 🚨 The Incident Report\n\nThe school library's website has arrived in a mess. When you open it, the page has no colours and the logo is missing. Here is the folder:\n\n```text\nlibrary-site\n├── index.html\n├── style.css      ◄── in the wrong place\n├── logo.png       ◄── in the wrong place\n├── js\n│   └── script.js\n└── backup\n    └── old_styles.css\n```\n\n## 🔎 Investigation\n\n### Step 1 and 2: Read and locate\n\nGo to the folder and confirm where you are.\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Amina>cd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site\n\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site>cd\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site\n```\n\n### Step 3: List the evidence\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site>dir /b\nbackup\nindex.html\njs\nlogo.png\nstyle.css\n```\n\n### Step 4: Compare with what the page expects\n\nPrint the page to see which paths it uses:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site>type index.html\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"css/style.css\">\n<img src=\"assets/logo.png\" alt=\"Library logo\">\n<script src=\"js/script.js\"></script>\n```\n\nThe page looks for `css/style.css` and `assets/logo.png`. But `style.css` and `logo.png` are lying in the main folder, and the `css` and `assets` folders do not exist. The browser cannot find them, so it shows no styles and no logo. `js/script.js` is correct.\n\n**Diagnosis**: two files are in the wrong place. The paths in the page are fine.\n\n## 🔧 The Fix in CMD\n\n```text\n:: Step 5a: make the missing folders\nmkdir css assets\n\n:: Step 5b: move each file to its home\nmove style.css css\\\nmove logo.png assets\\\n\n:: Step 5c: look inside backup BEFORE removing it\ndir /b backup\n```\n\n`dir /b backup` shows only `old_styles.css`, an old copy nobody needs. Now remove the folder, and answer the question:\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site>rmdir /s backup\nbackup, Are you sure (Y/N)? Y\n```\n\nWe list first and let CMD ask. We do not add `/q`.\n\n## 🔧 The Fix in PowerShell\n\n```text\nmkdir css, assets\nmove style.css css\\\nmove logo.png assets\\\ndir -Name backup\nrmdir backup\n```\n\nBecause `backup` has a file inside, PowerShell shows its **Confirm** question. Read the path, then answer `Y`.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Git Bash / macOS**:\n> ```text\n> mkdir css assets\n> mv style.css css/\n> mv logo.png assets/\n> ls backup\n> rm -r -i backup\n> ```\n> `-i` asks before each item is removed.\n\n## ✅ Step 6: Check\n\n```text\nC:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\library-site>tree /f\nFolder PATH listing\nVolume serial number is 1A2B-3C4D\nC:.\n│   index.html\n│\n├───assets\n│       logo.png\n│\n├───css\n│       style.css\n│\n└───js\n        script.js\n```\n\nEvery path in `index.html` now matches a real file. Start a server with `php -S localhost:8000` and open the page. The colours and logo are back! The log now shows `[200]` for `css/style.css` and `assets/logo.png`. Stop the server with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd>.\n\n## 🎓 What You Have Mastered\n\nLook how far you have come:\n\n- **Finding your way**: `cd`, `cd ..`, `cd \\`, `pwd`, drives, relative and absolute paths.\n- **Looking around**: `dir`, `ls`, `dir /a`, `tree /f`, `cls`.\n- **Building**: `mkdir`, `type nul >`, `New-Item`, `echo` with `>` and `>>`.\n- **Organising**: `ren`, `move`, `copy`, `xcopy`.\n- **Safe deleting**: list first, `del /p`, `rmdir`, `rmdir /s` with a question.\n- **Developer work**: `code .`, `--version`, `where`, `php -S`, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd>, `type`, `findstr`, pipes.\n\nYou are ready for the **Git & GitHub** book (`book-git/`). Git is used entirely through the terminal you now know.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Fixing before investigating.** A beginner sees the missing logo and edits `index.html`, changing `assets/logo.png` to `logo.png`. The logo appears. But now the page does not match the project's plan, and the next teammate is confused. A good detective finds the real cause first: the files were in the wrong place.\n\n**Removing a folder without looking inside.** `backup` might have held the only copy of something important. Always `dir` a folder before `rmdir /s`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nRecreate the messy `library-site` folder in your practice folder, using Chapters 7 and 12. Then solve the case yourself, without looking at the solution. Time how long the six steps take you.\n\n*Hint*: For `index.html`, write the three lines with `echo` and `>>`. In CMD, put `^` before each `<` and `>`.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Troubleshooting is a routine: read, locate, list, compare, fix, check.\n- Error messages are clues. Most mean a typo, a wrong folder, or a missing file.\n- Broken links on a page usually mean a file is not where the path says.\n- Look inside a folder before removing it, and let the terminal ask you to confirm.\n- Always check your fix: with `tree /f`, `dir`, and the browser.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\n1. Name the six steps of the detective routine.\n2. What does `The system cannot find the path specified.` usually mean?\n3. What does `[404]` mean in a server log?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. Read, locate, list, compare, fix, check.\n2. A folder in the path you typed does not exist where you are (or it is spelled wrongly).\n3. The browser asked for a file that the server could not find.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nThe logo shows on your Windows computer. But on the school's web server, which runs Linux, the logo is missing. The server reports **404 Not Found** for `/images/school-logo.png`.\n\n`dir /b images` prints:\n\n```text\nSchool-Logo.PNG\n```\n\n1. What is the problem?\n2. Give a command that fixes it without changing the HTML.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. The file name does not match. The page asks for `school-logo.png` (all lowercase), but the file is `School-Logo.PNG`. Windows ignores capital letters in file names, so it worked at home. Linux treats them as different names, so the server cannot find the file.\n2. From the site folder: `ren images\\School-Logo.PNG school-logo.png`.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA new case arrives. The page `index.html` in `charity-site` contains:\n\n```html\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"css/main.css\">\n<script src=\"js/donate.js\"></script>\n```\n\nThe folder holds: `index.html`, `styles` (with `main.css` inside), and `donate.js`.\n\n1. List the clues and write your diagnosis.\n2. Write the commands (CMD or PowerShell) that fix the folder without editing `index.html`.\n3. Write the commands that check your fix.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. Clues: the page wants `css/main.css`, but the folder is called `styles`. The page wants `js/donate.js`, but there is no `js` folder; `donate.js` sits in the main folder. Diagnosis: one folder has the wrong name and one file is in the wrong place.\n2. Fix:\n\n```text\ncd C:\\JuniorCoders\\practice\\charity-site\nren styles css\nmkdir js\nmove donate.js js\\\n```\n\n3. Check:\n\n```text\ntree /f\nphp -S localhost:8000\n```\n\nOpen `http://localhost:8000`. The log should show `[200]` for `css/main.css` and `js/donate.js`. Stop the server with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd>.\n\n</details>\n"
};
